"repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/7244e9f014cddc6f3349d4d07c31add8eb6e0ab0/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T09:22:31Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T09:22:31Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(hot): send endless retries to the client as a number it keeps (#2469)\n\nThe entry query is JSON, and JSON writes Infinity as null. The client dropped\nthat and fell back to its default, so a server asking never to give up\nreconnecting got ten retries instead.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"46946189a614adda86593322dd7f4f011daa8de5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/46946189a614adda86593322dd7f4f011daa8de5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/7244e9f014cddc6f3349d4d07c31add8eb6e0ab0","verification":{"payload":"tree 46946189a614adda86593322dd7f4f011daa8de5\nparent 39ca955ae3f2ff4a58d5bf232b0c768add45ef56\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1791278551 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791278551 +0300\n\nfix(hot): send endless retries to the client as a number it keeps (#2469)\n\nThe entry query is JSON, and JSON writes Infinity as null. The client dropped\nthat and fell back to its default, so a server asking never to give up\nreconnecting got ten retries instead.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxL3XCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAABbkQAKR9d/a9D3+gKyKqNdl+QLRL\ntJ8cakjpy3czZJOR19H6D/WTNfM0kJFYaFH7nqiIXCiOztrtzKzY1rhw5O9Lwft7\npNjOI1Qa/NbRY81Vd8ymu8kC6OMTamvn6bV+wkKgwH8kjCHUcVA9YbIm3mLnxT+t\nLZ67MaQ1+7KZXPpHVghHg08XmW3MUsOzW7a4z2Aa7Y5s5hyYmeuDVybPNXz0vtDf\nVupeYvccXKo8ynJC+IUq3nJ5J2hWcIMTuGyGERyuhYazoeg3VekPS5dfrZ+q60ek\n9OF9HfWoyif0iILCKSxh7/fOXw26Agl+KtIfk9Ep5Ja7u9Fni/87JFRu99Pnr1Fq\nORhmONHcjn1u+gD0+/YEPcb4e2QaNT7t9CSQqJRSwyCWO/vEGBbA+NUrfCPyd64/\nA/woAbawu7206LB2lQ3GoVgcjW/xrm+38PbW0ZMWLQxUdS9I2w7uzQ1dYSuM/qjo\nGF2SwZ0HGukn1DLRhd3SUVt3OsUUul01gW6rzg1Y1+7OZBaRTzg30qsHWkXfzXNm\nIcN14IYKQVDBJ5RTEHK7KszoVgLUFhT7uuemaJzvBe6JrN8NlaBcR30Go0wGl042\n89q14kWof+GSx0oxJbWenANkuUbfoxX4SmWgLw170EJfMYv2sTtySVEalTLavsD+\nidv/sV2394757tFYey+r\n=koUg\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T09:22:32Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/7244e9f014cddc6f3349d4d07c31add8eb6e0ab0","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDcyNDRlOWYwMTRjZGRjNmYzMzQ5ZDRkMDdjMzFhZGQ4ZWI2ZTBhYjA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/39ca955ae3f2ff4a58d5bf232b0c768add45ef56","sha":"39ca955ae3f2ff4a58d5bf232b0c768add45ef56","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/39ca955ae3f2ff4a58d5bf232b0c768add45ef56"}],"sha":"7244e9f014cddc6f3349d4d07c31add8eb6e0ab0","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/7244e9f014cddc6f3349d4d07c31add8eb6e0ab0"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/39ca955ae3f2ff4a58d5bf232b0c768add45ef56/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-06T02:08:52Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-06T02:08:52Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(hot): let the client say whose runtime it is in the console (#2462)\n\n* feat(hot): let the client say whose runtime it is in the console\n\n`hot.client.logging` takes an object as well as a level, carrying the name\nevery message is labelled with:\n\n    hot: { client: { logging: { level: \"warn\", name: \"my-dev-server\" } } }\n\nThe reason is the one that made the overlay''s element id an option. The\npackage a developer installed is the one they would report a problem to, and a\nconsole labelled with a dependency''s name sends them to the wrong repository.\nIt matters most where this runtime is the whole of another package''s client —\nwebpack-dev-server, whose users have read `[webpack-dev-server]` for years and\nwould otherwise start reading this package''s name instead.\n\nUnset, it is this package''s name as before.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(hot): read the logging object by what it parses to\n\nThe second shape was detected by testing the first character for `{`, so json\nwith a space before it fell through: the object text became the level and the\nname was lost. Asking what `JSON.parse` returned instead covers that, and\ncovers more than trimming would — `JSON.parse` also accepts a bare number,\nboolean or quoted string, and a level is none of those, so requiring an object\nis the check that was meant.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(types): let the exported logging type take the object as well\n\nThe runtime schema accepted `{ level, name }` while the generated `.d.ts` only\naccepted a level, so a TypeScript configuration setting the object form got a\ntype error for something that works. The level is a `LogLevel` alias now,\nwhich both halves of the union share.\n\nAlso a case the two browser tests could not catch: both used `\"info\"`, the\ndefault, so the object''s `name` being applied while its `level` was dropped\nwould have passed them. The new one asks for `\"warn\"` and requires `connected`\n— logged at info — to be absent, so the level has to come from inside the\nobject. Checked by breaking it on purpose: it fails.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"10b3ac51b75a9cea9366709f99af761d0b8e0cad","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/10b3ac51b75a9cea9366709f99af761d0b8e0cad"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/39ca955ae3f2ff4a58d5bf232b0c768add45ef56","verification":{"payload":"tree 10b3ac51b75a9cea9366709f99af761d0b8e0cad\nparent 48f139c476b323a5c7fd39f51dd6d11d059931b9\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1791252532 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791252532 +0300\n\nfeat(hot): let the client say whose runtime it is in the console (#2462)\n\n* feat(hot): let the client say whose runtime it is in the console\n\n`hot.client.logging` takes an object as well as a level, carrying the name\nevery message is labelled with:\n\n    hot: { client: { logging: { level: \"warn\", name: \"my-dev-server\" } } }\n\nThe reason is the one that made the overlay''s element id an option. The\npackage a developer installed is the one they would report a problem to, and a\nconsole labelled with a dependency''s name sends them to the wrong repository.\nIt matters most where this runtime is the whole of another package''s client —\nwebpack-dev-server, whose users have read `[webpack-dev-server]` for years and\nwould otherwise start reading this package''s name instead.\n\nUnset, it is this package''s name as before.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(hot): read the logging object by what it parses to\n\nThe second shape was detected by testing the first character for `{`, so json\nwith a space before it fell through: the object text became the level and the\nname was lost. Asking what `JSON.parse` returned instead covers that, and\ncovers more than trimming would — `JSON.parse` also accepts a bare number,\nboolean or quoted string, and a level is none of those, so requiring an object\nis the check that was meant.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(types): let the exported logging type take the object as well\n\nThe runtime schema accepted `{ level, name }` while the generated `.d.ts` only\naccepted a level, so a TypeScript configuration setting the object form got a\ntype error for something that works. The level is a `LogLevel` alias now,\nwhich both halves of the union share.\n\nAlso a case the two browser tests could not catch: both used `\"info\"`, the\ndefault, so the object''s `name` being applied while its `level` was dropped\nwould have passed them. The new one asks for `\"warn\"` and requires `connected`\n— logged at info — to be absent, so the level has to come from inside the\nobject. Checked by breaking it on purpose: it fails.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxFg0CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAmVwQABAgF+Ks+KT1V5wKlgVbOrgb\na0c6o5YRQLlRdTkTnjlfe7w4VoCk1WoPUNL7Qk+r36t8DH/O+5PioXGAAtGJ9hHR\n6ZZOTZf2SWuKxiMI611pK0/Y4P11FqPPRusXjsdJlUTldRKZskyzC/aj22tNvKhu\nazG3p25Kpdbq60RWwRouMy+y6Cqm/F7fioO8HzNIke6iDbO/PVgEzrdtcuXfyh4t\nGQzRIV+BitY5G2H6aAnIEFZRpesmRSQcYftiD+KGLXhiGvP9J79c0yiBLp6DmXcy\n8vKLPBPpEHHgaZiF8tBDGwyuhpaPgDvevMRl8KjZ9VB2uFb5N1xpps3xrjMamU/I\nQYOvSdPoWpID7KgbePEWs/J99Fx6TW4SNiT7j6KxcDibgnhGnfVLjP8ZB6XssOW0\nUqu2SNhFo9h98kcsvPAhHguARD8JJLTu/JIqwcghgfQ6aiI03MLHQUHp0w8ubFkA\ntP9zk6kJFpKYHtRVZqvc7bF0pdeDcv+PVMGLy7jYx998syNrc0TvWFSiTmeTJsoB\ntkAEmC1qVMQrI5mbeeLAAEPpXKPIKXcdiWpVTETR4J4hhAlo0SXT9TGUXiGXiBa6\nVmdmTlLE006RMhAunN7Wt3ZYQtqIlvFYKHCUmhQUoe3rlXln10o7/xZlJhHEPuQJ\nsUSAZQyT3es6aQGoAHGi\n=XZzw\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-06T02:08:53Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/39ca955ae3f2ff4a58d5bf232b0c768add45ef56","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDM5Y2E5NTVhZTNmMmZmNGE1OGQ1YmYyMzJiMGM3NjhhZGQ0NWVmNTY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/48f139c476b323a5c7fd39f51dd6d11d059931b9","sha":"48f139c476b323a5c7fd39f51dd6d11d059931b9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/48f139c476b323a5c7fd39f51dd6d11d059931b9"}],"sha":"39ca955ae3f2ff4a58d5bf232b0c768add45ef56","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/39ca955ae3f2ff4a58d5bf232b0c768add45ef56"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/48f139c476b323a5c7fd39f51dd6d11d059931b9/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-05T19:58:15Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-05T19:58:15Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(hot): understand an `error` action from the server (#2461)\n\n* feat(hot): understand an `error` action from the server\n\nA server applying a policy of its own refuses a client and holds the only\nexplanation for it — webpack-dev-server''s `allowedHosts` is the case this\nexists for. There was nowhere to put that: an unknown action reaches a\n`subscribeAll` handler and otherwise goes nowhere, so the connection closed\nwith the reason nowhere the developer was looking.\n\n`{ action: \"error\", message }` is logged as the server gave it and posted to\nthe page as `webpackError`, so tooling watching the stream sees it too.\n\nNo policy comes with it. Which clients to refuse, and why, stays entirely the\nserver''s; this is only somewhere to say so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* feat(hot): expose `publishTo`, for answering one client\n\nAn `error` action is for the one client being refused, and the README example\nfor it reached for `instance.publishTo?.()` — which the transports have and\nthe instance did not, so the optional call did nothing and the client closed\nwith no explanation after all. The method is on the instance now, beside\n`publish`.\n\nWriting to the client directly is not the alternative: a WebSocket client and\nthe event stream''s `ServerResponse` are not the same kind of object, and\nneither one''s write method exists on the other, so a message sent the wrong\nway silently never arrives. `publishTo` takes a client of either and puts the\npayload on the wire that client is on.\n\nThe example also closed every client with `client.close()`, which a\n`ServerResponse` does not have. It tells the two apart now, and the method is\ndocumented with the same distinction.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(types): let `publishTo` carry a payload of your own too\n\nMissed when this branch was rebased onto `main`: `publishTo` was written\nagainst `{ action: string }` while `publish` had already moved to\n`CustomPayload`, so the method meant for answering one client — a refusal,\ncarrying whatever the server has to say about it — rejected the extra fields\nits own forwarding target accepts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"9de629357490b1471d9a1915e7ee261733ae5a28","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/9de629357490b1471d9a1915e7ee261733ae5a28"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/48f139c476b323a5c7fd39f51dd6d11d059931b9","verification":{"payload":"tree 9de629357490b1471d9a1915e7ee261733ae5a28\nparent 5c5cf728f22c82bc7c70f7845e8a0b24e474a62d\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1791230295 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791230295 +0300\n\nfeat(hot): understand an `error` action from the server (#2461)\n\n* feat(hot): understand an `error` action from the server\n\nA server applying a policy of its own refuses a client and holds the only\nexplanation for it — webpack-dev-server''s `allowedHosts` is the case this\nexists for. There was nowhere to put that: an unknown action reaches a\n`subscribeAll` handler and otherwise goes nowhere, so the connection closed\nwith the reason nowhere the developer was looking.\n\n`{ action: \"error\", message }` is logged as the server gave it and posted to\nthe page as `webpackError`, so tooling watching the stream sees it too.\n\nNo policy comes with it. Which clients to refuse, and why, stays entirely the\nserver''s; this is only somewhere to say so.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* feat(hot): expose `publishTo`, for answering one client\n\nAn `error` action is for the one client being refused, and the README example\nfor it reached for `instance.publishTo?.()` — which the transports have and\nthe instance did not, so the optional call did nothing and the client closed\nwith no explanation after all. The method is on the instance now, beside\n`publish`.\n\nWriting to the client directly is not the alternative: a WebSocket client and\nthe event stream''s `ServerResponse` are not the same kind of object, and\nneither one''s write method exists on the other, so a message sent the wrong\nway silently never arrives. `publishTo` takes a client of either and puts the\npayload on the wire that client is on.\n\nThe example also closed every client with `client.close()`, which a\n`ServerResponse` does not have. It tells the two apart now, and the method is\ndocumented with the same distinction.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(types): let `publishTo` carry a payload of your own too\n\nMissed when this branch was rebased onto `main`: `publishTo` was written\nagainst `{ action: string }` while `publish` had already moved to\n`CustomPayload`, so the method meant for answering one client — a refusal,\ncarrying whatever the server has to say about it — rejected the extra fields\nits own forwarding target accepts.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqxAFXCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAxfoQALGMl4G0TtLJKkHsEBE+NZ/z\nBIrU3pJRYNSapNYS7yrW3DTjdwMe7+QfBk6Eqwd4462+ZNIKolrEw9v7f5LiJ4L9\nQGyjgI5JeCSLSm2d6n+GXTR3z5wYa+acCfyQkFoR3JwJtRuz8IIjCVhhu1999Jpc\nglYWi5cQ7niXlJ6Dc75CUMM/IzrJeddilAn5xiqtuucThPNUAaeWaJQ2+AtxZFPl\nwCBNMKjUkNZFTPwDVQJZnPJ3RvaqkFxAU98xbe3i2ZVMrfGDYxqQdYJCgNKs2Pz+\nLKJnDUVR9qHdx3thCvEiW03cDVjYW+hqq0V6RYkhKNJKd5cU1vAuAHM8TgATEfBV\nw0ks3/5F1iJfUyt27+aJTAegdM/O++O+CbpAhdUVZamlPaxyNUSj7e00daZWgkea\nOS3hL8sIph5D/YKMoIARHs4B/tG9K4ButwZZyBCqL3sg2yQoN6VXnydHDzfWrHAW\nWARl+647WZ1ffiitYDeKnNBGF3EWvQ8okwRJyJeDkCJOk52U+B6/c1ibaZ84mjXc\nykDSOISnvX0mGGV+vqj+vJhfRtlw5X2AG+Dyo17AY9sK4B1Y0Q7LDNVWzSn79NYd\nr12jp0x8gASO0bP3VnLo52Tq0eY2+1hoGPbj5iM6465Q2LERT5aMNh7ipVyZKK9V\nRZTEYKDJbHvlJFCa8XcK\n=ce+i\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-05T19:58:15Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/48f139c476b323a5c7fd39f51dd6d11d059931b9","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDQ4ZjEzOWM0NzZiMzIzYTVjN2ZkMzlmNTFkZDZkMTFkMDU5OTMxYjk","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/5c5cf728f22c82bc7c70f7845e8a0b24e474a62d","sha":"5c5cf728f22c82bc7c70f7845e8a0b24e474a62d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/5c5cf728f22c82bc7c70f7845e8a0b24e474a62d"}],"sha":"48f139c476b323a5c7fd39f51dd6d11d059931b9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/48f139c476b323a5c7fd39f51dd6d11d059931b9"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/5c5cf728f22c82bc7c70f7845e8a0b24e474a62d/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-05T18:25:30Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-05T18:25:30Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(types): let `publish` carry a payload of your own (#2460)\n\n`publish` exists for what a server measures that this middleware does not, so\na payload of someone else''s carries whatever they have to say — but its type\nallowed only `{ action: string }` beside the keys published here, which made a\ntype error of a key that works perfectly well at runtime.\n\nwebpack-dev-server''s own `ProgressPlugin` is the case: it reports which plugin\na tick came from, which is more than `percent` and `message`, and a\n`subscribe` handler is what reads it. Typing it shut made the shape of a\npublished payload this middleware''s to approve, which is the opposite of what\nthe method is for.\n\n`action` stays required, being what a client tells one payload from another by.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"856fd65ddeedfe6a530ece893427a6bf43bc98ed","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/856fd65ddeedfe6a530ece893427a6bf43bc98ed"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/5c5cf728f22c82bc7c70f7845e8a0b24e474a62d","verification":{"payload":"tree 856fd65ddeedfe6a530ece893427a6bf43bc98ed\nparent c528079fbb052bbec720423c010e1cb9de93595e\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1791224730 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791224730 +0300\n\nfix(types): let `publish` carry a payload of your own (#2460)\n\n`publish` exists for what a server measures that this middleware does not, so\na payload of someone else''s carries whatever they have to say — but its type\nallowed only `{ action: string }` beside the keys published here, which made a\ntype error of a key that works perfectly well at runtime.\n\nwebpack-dev-server''s own `ProgressPlugin` is the case: it reports which plugin\na tick came from, which is more than `percent` and `message`, and a\n`subscribe` handler is what reads it. Typing it shut made the shape of a\npublished payload this middleware''s to approve, which is the opposite of what\nthe method is for.\n\n`action` stays required, being what a client tells one payload from another by.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqw+uaCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAOg0QAIcE8B/4lizyTa1UMMvstM1f\nxSw/xuFfK5rJ3ROJkdOj59LzBXMOpHno29GHhiJ0BcHoFigta4LYdEoksyL1OTaF\nr3ojgLNJQtz8Qpr1MYXeqGsKfHNsYKtjkO5t5uCg1/+LA6fq7Cv/Bp/zsM9k+d7Z\nGLp3grxmBRlNJC/OtNPJAWwPiwh15KVQgzkrDHUd2vmf2WKK4IIDxGFe1f/LqZBg\niPR4tZ1CG2LM09QIQtALydKF/s3DIkn2ZF4Lu6C0DyNOfaDn7tszxEGZNoic2Kdq\nKDJkcvUwU6dgio3GL0yEInmScgjBJdzcURL9oP31z5J9etIZcYjs9WZOneriJ25v\nSe9bXlDsGplcQgY/9NwUZEf2DC30HCswq4mnpMVYuG3GHqm39HvrRwCrtfaLCrsF\nuZKQfIHaceEodJm6rqOnqhxR+u7A/K/BPGWLpyfddk2Ow2kzfgJMJi/KLIaKT9yj\n0kN1X4VsqtEazrQx+vNnI95tZEnSLhtmCc9iJOfXNJzFGSicmiUBVjkZ3Jr2uysb\n3VGpvduahOSp6FXm0BHvxopcLf+fJr6k0e/3OcGVgCZQSqBZ8MTJkD8NqliQS3EO\nymlrKrOYRIW9c3vbz9YYK36NYA1WrdUIHcg7tYGC5UWCBFBlx8kg9TfCaTnC3lVU\npmV3KzXOgsPWnuDNLYXU\n=1aVS\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-05T18:25:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/5c5cf728f22c82bc7c70f7845e8a0b24e474a62d","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDVjNWNmNzI4ZjIyYzgyYmM3YzcwZjc4NDVlOGEwYjI0ZTQ3NGE2MmQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/c528079fbb052bbec720423c010e1cb9de93595e","sha":"c528079fbb052bbec720423c010e1cb9de93595e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/c528079fbb052bbec720423c010e1cb9de93595e"}],"sha":"5c5cf728f22c82bc7c70f7845e8a0b24e474a62d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/5c5cf728f22c82bc7c70f7845e8a0b24e474a62d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/c528079fbb052bbec720423c010e1cb9de93595e/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-05T16:07:06Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-05T16:07:06Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(hot): add `apply` and `connect`, replacing six browser options (#2458)\n\n* feat(hot)!: replace six browser options with `apply` and `connect`\n\nTwo unions, both of options that shipped after 8.3.0 — so nothing released\nhas to keep working and no aliases are needed.\n\n`apply` replaces `hot`, `liveReload` and `reload`. Only four of their eight\ncombinations ever differed, which the control flow says plainly:\n`liveReload` is read only in the `else` branch of `if (hot)`, and `reload`\nonly inside `applyUpdate`, which the `if` branch calls. Three booleans for\none decision.\n\n    hot: true,  reload: true      -> apply: \"hmr\"\n    hot: true,  reload: false     -> apply: \"hmr-only\"\n    hot: false, liveReload: true  -> apply: \"reload\"\n    hot: false, liveReload: false -> apply: \"nothing\"\n\n`connect` replaces `autoConnect`, `reconnect` and `timeout`: `false` does\nnot connect on load, and an object carries `retries` and `timeout`. The\nper-transport semantics are unchanged, still in `socket-options.js`, which\nis why `connect` could land without re-deriving them.\n\nThe page-url parameter follows `apply` and gains from it: it used to be\nable only to turn something off, so `-hot=false` and `-liveReload=false`\nwere two switches with a gap between them. `-apply=<mode>` asks for an\noutcome, and `=false` is still taken as `nothing` because that is what the\nold pair meant together.\n\nBoth are the shape this codebase already uses everywhere — `overlay`,\n`token`, `cors`, `progress` and `transport` are all a scalar that may be an\nobject — so neither reads as an exception.\n\nTwo things found on the way. The symmetry test could not see a type with an\nobject literal in it: `[^}]+` stopped at the brace inside\n`{ retries?: number }`, so `connect` looked absent from the typedef. And\none live-reload case had combined a recognised `hot=false` with an\nunrecognised `live-reload=false`; with one option that scenario cannot\nexist, so it now asserts what it was really about — an unrecognised value\nleaves the default in force.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* test(e2e): map the last opt-out fixture to the right mode\n\n`-hot=false` turned Hot Module Replacement off and left live reload on, so\nthe build reached the page as a reload. I had mapped it to\n`-apply=nothing`, which is the one mode where the build reaches the page\nnot at all — so the test waited thirty seconds for text that was never\ngoing to change. `reload` is the mode that behaviour had a name for.\n\nThe equivalent fixture in `live-reload.test.js` was mapped correctly, which\nis what made the difference visible.\n\nAlso drops the snapshot left behind by a renamed test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* feat(hot): keep the six names `apply` and `connect` replaced working\n\nNot a breaking change any more: `hot`, `liveReload`, `reload`,\n`autoConnect`, `reconnect` and `timeout` all still apply, and are folded\ninto the two options that replaced them.\n\n    hot: true,  reload: true      -> apply: \"hmr\"\n    hot: true,  reload: false     -> apply: \"hmr-only\"\n    hot: false, liveReload: true  -> apply: \"reload\"\n    hot: false, liveReload: false -> apply: \"nothing\"\n    autoConnect: false            -> connect: false\n    reconnect, timeout            -> connect: { retries, timeout }\n\nThe new option wins when both are set: the other way round, a migration\nthat sets it and leaves the old name behind would silently not apply. Each\nold name warns, and it has to warn in two places — node, when it is set on\n`hot.client`, and the browser, when it arrives on the query, since an entry\nsomebody wired by hand never passes through the node side at all.\n\nThe folding happens in the browser rather than in `clientQuery` for the\nsame reason: a hand-written query has to work, and node cannot translate\none it never sees.\n\nThe symmetry test needed to learn about this. The deprecated names are read\nfrom a list rather than written out one by one, so `overrides.hot` does not\nappear in the source for it to find; it now collects them from\n`LEGACY_OPTIONS`, with an assertion on the length of that list so a\nregex that stops matching cannot quietly shrink the comparison.\n\nFive browser tests cover the folding, including the precedence rule and\nboth warning channels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs(hot): document `apply` and `connect` in the client options table\n\nBoth are listed as the replacement the six deprecated rows point to, but the\ntable had no row for either, so following those links left you without their\nvalues, default or object shape. The `urlPrefix` row still described the\nparameter as turning `hot` and `liveReload` off, which is the shape it had\nbefore `apply` replaced them.\n\nRebasing onto main resolved every conflicting hunk to the legacy side, which\nalso dropped content that happened to share those hunks: these rows, the\n`urlPrefix` wording, and three tests covering the shapes `connect` takes.\nRestored, and the table now reads live options first with the six deprecated\nnames together at the end.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs(changeset): name the current options in the earlier entries\n\nAll of these land in one release, so the changelog is read by someone who only\never sees the final names. Three entries described options and page-url\nparameters as they were partway through: the retry count and silence timeout\nunder their replaced names, a `-liveReload=false` parameter that no longer\nexists under any name, and a claim that every option has exactly one spelling,\nwhich the six kept for a release contradict. The replaced names are noted where\nthey were the subject, since they still work.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(hot): send an object-valued browser option as json\n\n`clientQuery` special-cased `overlay` and ran `String(value)` over everything\nelse, which was true of every other option until `connect` took an object too.\n`{ retries: 3, timeout: 5000 }` became the text `\"[object Object]\"`; the client\nparses `connect` as JSON, the parse threw, and the fallback read the text as a\nboolean — so both fields were dropped in silence and the connection ran on the\ndefaults.\n\nThe rule is now the value''s shape rather than the option''s name: an object goes\nover as JSON, `overlay` keeping the encoding its filter functions need. The e2e\ntests set `connect` through a hand-written entry query, which skips this path\nentirely, so the new one sets it on the middleware and watches for the\nshortened watchdog interval actually arriving — it fails on `String(value)`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs(hot): say that only `apply` can be set from a page''s url\n\nThe client options section read as though every option could be set in three\nplaces. Two is right: the middleware and the entry query, with the same effect\nand the same spelling. A page''s own url reaches exactly one option, `apply`,\nso `?webpack-dev-middleware-connect=false` does not stop a page connecting —\nit is not read at all. The `urlPrefix` row and the section it links to were\nalready singular; this is the paragraph above them, and the same sentence in\nan earlier changeset.\n\nPinned with a test rather than left to the prose, since being wrong about\n`connect` in particular would be a page that quietly stops receiving builds.\nIt fails if the parameter is swapped for `-apply=nothing`, so it is asserting\nthe boundary and not just that the page works.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"1137c7594ad4b4d5f1261bdbacf16f4edce6da30","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/1137c7594ad4b4d5f1261bdbacf16f4edce6da30"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/c528079fbb052bbec720423c010e1cb9de93595e","verification":{"payload":"tree 1137c7594ad4b4d5f1261bdbacf16f4edce6da30\nparent 792bb6118b6b1b4d6ab4da6d09d974aa564a6710\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1791216426 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791216426 +0300\n\nfeat(hot): add `apply` and `connect`, replacing six browser options (#2458)\n\n* feat(hot)!: replace six browser options with `apply` and `connect`\n\nTwo unions, both of options that shipped after 8.3.0 — so nothing released\nhas to keep working and no aliases are needed.\n\n`apply` replaces `hot`, `liveReload` and `reload`. Only four of their eight\ncombinations ever differed, which the control flow says plainly:\n`liveReload` is read only in the `else` branch of `if (hot)`, and `reload`\nonly inside `applyUpdate`, which the `if` branch calls. Three booleans for\none decision.\n\n    hot: true,  reload: true      -> apply: \"hmr\"\n    hot: true,  reload: false     -> apply: \"hmr-only\"\n    hot: false, liveReload: true  -> apply: \"reload\"\n    hot: false, liveReload: false -> apply: \"nothing\"\n\n`connect` replaces `autoConnect`, `reconnect` and `timeout`: `false` does\nnot connect on load, and an object carries `retries` and `timeout`. The\nper-transport semantics are unchanged, still in `socket-options.js`, which\nis why `connect` could land without re-deriving them.\n\nThe page-url parameter follows `apply` and gains from it: it used to be\nable only to turn something off, so `-hot=false` and `-liveReload=false`\nwere two switches with a gap between them. `-apply=<mode>` asks for an\noutcome, and `=false` is still taken as `nothing` because that is what the\nold pair meant together.\n\nBoth are the shape this codebase already uses everywhere — `overlay`,\n`token`, `cors`, `progress` and `transport` are all a scalar that may be an\nobject — so neither reads as an exception.\n\nTwo things found on the way. The symmetry test could not see a type with an\nobject literal in it: `[^}]+` stopped at the brace inside\n`{ retries?: number }`, so `connect` looked absent from the typedef. And\none live-reload case had combined a recognised `hot=false` with an\nunrecognised `live-reload=false`; with one option that scenario cannot\nexist, so it now asserts what it was really about — an unrecognised value\nleaves the default in force.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* test(e2e): map the last opt-out fixture to the right mode\n\n`-hot=false` turned Hot Module Replacement off and left live reload on, so\nthe build reached the page as a reload. I had mapped it to\n`-apply=nothing`, which is the one mode where the build reaches the page\nnot at all — so the test waited thirty seconds for text that was never\ngoing to change. `reload` is the mode that behaviour had a name for.\n\nThe equivalent fixture in `live-reload.test.js` was mapped correctly, which\nis what made the difference visible.\n\nAlso drops the snapshot left behind by a renamed test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* feat(hot): keep the six names `apply` and `connect` replaced working\n\nNot a breaking change any more: `hot`, `liveReload`, `reload`,\n`autoConnect`, `reconnect` and `timeout` all still apply, and are folded\ninto the two options that replaced them.\n\n    hot: true,  reload: true      -> apply: \"hmr\"\n    hot: true,  reload: false     -> apply: \"hmr-only\"\n    hot: false, liveReload: true  -> apply: \"reload\"\n    hot: false, liveReload: false -> apply: \"nothing\"\n    autoConnect: false            -> connect: false\n    reconnect, timeout            -> connect: { retries, timeout }\n\nThe new option wins when both are set: the other way round, a migration\nthat sets it and leaves the old name behind would silently not apply. Each\nold name warns, and it has to warn in two places — node, when it is set on\n`hot.client`, and the browser, when it arrives on the query, since an entry\nsomebody wired by hand never passes through the node side at all.\n\nThe folding happens in the browser rather than in `clientQuery` for the\nsame reason: a hand-written query has to work, and node cannot translate\none it never sees.\n\nThe symmetry test needed to learn about this. The deprecated names are read\nfrom a list rather than written out one by one, so `overrides.hot` does not\nappear in the source for it to find; it now collects them from\n`LEGACY_OPTIONS`, with an assertion on the length of that list so a\nregex that stops matching cannot quietly shrink the comparison.\n\nFive browser tests cover the folding, including the precedence rule and\nboth warning channels.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs(hot): document `apply` and `connect` in the client options table\n\nBoth are listed as the replacement the six deprecated rows point to, but the\ntable had no row for either, so following those links left you without their\nvalues, default or object shape. The `urlPrefix` row still described the\nparameter as turning `hot` and `liveReload` off, which is the shape it had\nbefore `apply` replaced them.\n\nRebasing onto main resolved every conflicting hunk to the legacy side, which\nalso dropped content that happened to share those hunks: these rows, the\n`urlPrefix` wording, and three tests covering the shapes `connect` takes.\nRestored, and the table now reads live options first with the six deprecated\nnames together at the end.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs(changeset): name the current options in the earlier entries\n\nAll of these land in one release, so the changelog is read by someone who only\never sees the final names. Three entries described options and page-url\nparameters as they were partway through: the retry count and silence timeout\nunder their replaced names, a `-liveReload=false` parameter that no longer\nexists under any name, and a claim that every option has exactly one spelling,\nwhich the six kept for a release contradict. The replaced names are noted where\nthey were the subject, since they still work.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(hot): send an object-valued browser option as json\n\n`clientQuery` special-cased `overlay` and ran `String(value)` over everything\nelse, which was true of every other option until `connect` took an object too.\n`{ retries: 3, timeout: 5000 }` became the text `\"[object Object]\"`; the client\nparses `connect` as JSON, the parse threw, and the fallback read the text as a\nboolean — so both fields were dropped in silence and the connection ran on the\ndefaults.\n\nThe rule is now the value''s shape rather than the option''s name: an object goes\nover as JSON, `overlay` keeping the encoding its filter functions need. The e2e\ntests set `connect` through a hand-written entry query, which skips this path\nentirely, so the new one sets it on the middleware and watches for the\nshortened watchdog interval actually arriving — it fails on `String(value)`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs(hot): say that only `apply` can be set from a page''s url\n\nThe client options section read as though every option could be set in three\nplaces. Two is right: the middleware and the entry query, with the same effect\nand the same spelling. A page''s own url reaches exactly one option, `apply`,\nso `?webpack-dev-middleware-connect=false` does not stop a page connecting —\nit is not read at all. The `urlPrefix` row and the section it links to were\nalready singular; this is the paragraph above them, and the same sentence in\nan earlier changeset.\n\nPinned with a test rather than left to the prose, since being wrong about\n`connect` in particular would be a page that quietly stops receiving builds.\nIt fails if the parameter is swapped for `-apply=nothing`, so it is asserting\nthe boundary and not just that the page works.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqw8sqCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA/xIQAJE5aHWEWAw8wpfYpZvbSJNI\nGYRbIvp+/KdP0BKFAmFN1jTpZm/SRQNMq6CtZhPnlYSlWvQcWIX6qHmzx2WcoDud\n/3FichnZ08W2VdlewTEoz/GsSHXJYQ8i2kia/+TLZm4k/VoHlnRk7AqVgo/C1tYl\n4nuCTUyub41eL6+Z5betq4QMIL4htN2n0EHhd3VfRCTDx7h51MAwZIDpLT4duOgF\n4f+Qj1czFJZHcqyjQJR8G91qOLk5BmiPce+j4krOeT/Yg/2uChmPcXWj/tVuvZYq\nvX4eZZ6QQlmxZMf6cg67IT4GgqzyPuFfjPbRHJZHWe+PFXyusgGKWL5Q/mwMo+9z\numrFbaglUbLAKcYk+oAQVOAXO5CNVH1GHlrsOMYa8qk43O6yrlti/Lrdy5Nz7cTF\nSjj+L+sYM4aH6EuK25JlTXzPkDzl0aAYkm6RnWASoNdKmC+viigjgBT0vGKjMqNH\nALBRJWb3bCmPGfpQBDNGpaZYv3+zl8F+hxcF6C26g0nvsLWfPasAbZTv2mz5KjQw\nRkXtajVncjTNg8t2EnQA/pZBYmukVOjwEpkT1YnE+40ml6Z5qWKdywZlz4UdAl+L\nNhAAGRbpEXNJRgGyImQZDR0o8VoLince6FAwA/bumze4Pp6G3yw9ZEQFHzkBLJ36\nNW6ERIdc8du5sB9CAhGc\n=Cjnh\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-05T16:07:09Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/c528079fbb052bbec720423c010e1cb9de93595e","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKGM1MjgwNzlmYmIwNTJiYmVjNzIwNDIzYzAxMGUxY2I5ZGU5MzU5NWU","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/792bb6118b6b1b4d6ab4da6d09d974aa564a6710","sha":"792bb6118b6b1b4d6ab4da6d09d974aa564a6710","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/792bb6118b6b1b4d6ab4da6d09d974aa564a6710"}],"sha":"c528079fbb052bbec720423c010e1cb9de93595e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/c528079fbb052bbec720423c010e1cb9de93595e"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/792bb6118b6b1b4d6ab4da6d09d974aa564a6710/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-03T10:06:58Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-03T10:06:58Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(client): make `reconnect` work on Server-Sent Events, and say where `timeout` applies (#2457)\n\n* fix(client): make `reconnect` work on Server-Sent Events, and say where `timeout` applies\n\nTwo options were each inert on one transport.\n\n`reconnect` was overridden to `Infinity` over Server-Sent Events, so asking\nfor a bounded number of attempts on the default transport did nothing. It\nis honoured now. Unset still means keep trying for as long as the page is\nopen: a dev server is expected to come back, and a tab left open across a\nrestart has to find it again.\n\n`timeout` was handed to a WebSocket client whose constructor is\n`constructor(url)` and takes no options at all. The fix there is not to\nimplement the watchdog, which was my first instinct and is wrong — the two\ntransports send different kinds of heartbeat:\n\n    EventSourceServer   client.write(\"data: 💓\\n\\n\")   data, visible to JS\n    WebSocketServer     client.ping(() => {})          a protocol ping\n\nThe browser answers a ping in its network stack and never shows it to\nJavaScript, so a silence watchdog over a WebSocket would fire on a\nconnection that is healthy and merely idle. The half-open case it would\nhave caught is already handled where the pong is visible: the server sees\nthe missing one and terminates the socket, so the browser gets a real\n`close` and reconnects. So `timeout` is Server-Sent Events only, it is\ndocumented as such, and it is no longer passed to a client that drops it.\n\nBoth decisions moved into `client-src/utils/socket-options.js`, which is a\npure function and therefore testable — eight cases, one per transport per\noption, including `reconnect: 0` meaning none rather than unset.\n\nThe README already said `\"sse\"` ignored `reconnect`; the schema did not.\nBoth say what happens now.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: give `reconnect` the default each transport actually has\n\nThe README quoted `10`, which is `createSocket`''s fallback and therefore\nonly what a WebSocket does. Unset over Server-Sent Events the retry count\nis `Infinity` — the figure was wrong for the default transport, and had\nbeen while the option was ignored there, so making it work is what made the\ndocumentation wrong rather than merely incomplete.\n\n    unset, \"sse\"  ->  Infinity   (this module''s default)\n    unset, \"ws\"   ->  10         (createSocket''s, reached by passing nothing)\n    set           ->  both\n\nA test pins both, reading `createSocket`''s number out of its source rather\nthan restating it, since that is the half the docs do not own.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c14adbdaaf507885b381904c905d71b65e1fddf4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/c14adbdaaf507885b381904c905d71b65e1fddf4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/792bb6118b6b1b4d6ab4da6d09d974aa564a6710","verification":{"payload":"tree c14adbdaaf507885b381904c905d71b65e1fddf4\nparent d0bc1fcef90aa635551ee33007aab6f78b498a80\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1791022018 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791022018 +0300\n\nfix(client): make `reconnect` work on Server-Sent Events, and say where `timeout` applies (#2457)\n\n* fix(client): make `reconnect` work on Server-Sent Events, and say where `timeout` applies\n\nTwo options were each inert on one transport.\n\n`reconnect` was overridden to `Infinity` over Server-Sent Events, so asking\nfor a bounded number of attempts on the default transport did nothing. It\nis honoured now. Unset still means keep trying for as long as the page is\nopen: a dev server is expected to come back, and a tab left open across a\nrestart has to find it again.\n\n`timeout` was handed to a WebSocket client whose constructor is\n`constructor(url)` and takes no options at all. The fix there is not to\nimplement the watchdog, which was my first instinct and is wrong — the two\ntransports send different kinds of heartbeat:\n\n    EventSourceServer   client.write(\"data: 💓\\n\\n\")   data, visible to JS\n    WebSocketServer     client.ping(() => {})          a protocol ping\n\nThe browser answers a ping in its network stack and never shows it to\nJavaScript, so a silence watchdog over a WebSocket would fire on a\nconnection that is healthy and merely idle. The half-open case it would\nhave caught is already handled where the pong is visible: the server sees\nthe missing one and terminates the socket, so the browser gets a real\n`close` and reconnects. So `timeout` is Server-Sent Events only, it is\ndocumented as such, and it is no longer passed to a client that drops it.\n\nBoth decisions moved into `client-src/utils/socket-options.js`, which is a\npure function and therefore testable — eight cases, one per transport per\noption, including `reconnect: 0` meaning none rather than unset.\n\nThe README already said `\"sse\"` ignored `reconnect`; the schema did not.\nBoth say what happens now.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: give `reconnect` the default each transport actually has\n\nThe README quoted `10`, which is `createSocket`''s fallback and therefore\nonly what a WebSocket does. Unset over Server-Sent Events the retry count\nis `Infinity` — the figure was wrong for the default transport, and had\nbeen while the option was ignored there, so making it work is what made the\ndocumentation wrong rather than merely incomplete.\n\n    unset, \"sse\"  ->  Infinity   (this module''s default)\n    unset, \"ws\"   ->  10         (createSocket''s, reached by passing nothing)\n    set           ->  both\n\nA test pins both, reading `createSocket`''s number out of its source rather\nthan restating it, since that is the half the docs do not own.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqwNPCCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAem0QAFiApQU+TW1KBDEu1kMzdcf/\nxiR6yN3DXeOGw70Ce5BZK3xu4MgqLRW65bNkeDvfBMTIGxqzQlTHjZLN7oT8MiRF\nPzMmCMx/CLOd21Jd66VpOE25KWIKKQPTTEh/jrp14K810z0LLJGcdHiJX1syhX1r\nSgPx76c1VhhRPnyRaiIjvC30W69CGoLbL3oub9zokC1SeMkhqlxWM0ixbQTv+ub/\nvuwCFuO4ayw3gBhKCheCMJkVESmcCY3bO11o91D23b/XVdwMxbWWpq7QSU4f0jWC\nu5pl+eEQWSQF4dnyhhOpKCcNtAlav1U56xkA9D4//lTZIByFh5J1aHEk/7MPS7Cu\n7S0LGIGTcZDuJ7N+1SMoPUUtgcsWiSnMrWNwQ4DW45KAF3QmBsypq/U5NhSAYtvV\n+2gtBxxbAjYdQ10KEz5HtJ6YlueBRJkU5RjtqdOiw/Kt5GCsnNAD78imJe3Ob7wk\nCkSW050EtIuD8pLGJe+IcSjxQeBb28NXULtvnpP3JKRsS6BAy1ukerSoH1P5lFt4\nPZ+uzGbSH+YGi74LIY7eLvEzevdvLB23GzIZN3WuVhc8m+cqHoZNw3PNSqWC/ZNM\nwLTsLI42V8nPlvTZdzTmE47Pv2+IplQItklHXm7WSyw23rbbGBcDLY965qCPONjL\nC+vgHMs7OUTYcTdxfq8R\n=PH5l\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-03T10:06:58Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/792bb6118b6b1b4d6ab4da6d09d974aa564a6710","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDc5MmJiNjExOGI2YjFiNGQ2YWI0ZGE2ZDA5ZDk3NGFhNTY0YTY3MTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/d0bc1fcef90aa635551ee33007aab6f78b498a80","sha":"d0bc1fcef90aa635551ee33007aab6f78b498a80","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/d0bc1fcef90aa635551ee33007aab6f78b498a80"}],"sha":"792bb6118b6b1b4d6ab4da6d09d974aa564a6710","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/792bb6118b6b1b4d6ab4da6d09d974aa564a6710"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/d0bc1fcef90aa635551ee33007aab6f78b498a80/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-03T10:06:39Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-03T10:06:39Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(client): read `autoConnect` off the query like every other boolean (#2456)\n\nIt tested `=== \"true\"` where the rest of them test `!== \"false\"`, so a\nvalue it did not recognise turned the client off:\n\n    ?autoConnect=1   -> was off, is now left on\n    ?hmr=1           -> left on, as it always was\n    ?autoConnect=false -> off, unchanged\n\nThe default is already on, so turning it off is the only thing anyone\nwrites this option for — which makes the odd reading a trap rather than a\nstricter policy.\n\nThe existing coverage could not catch it: it only used `autoConnect=false`,\nwhich both readings treat the same way. Two tests now, one for each half,\nand the first was checked against the old reading to be sure it fails\nthere.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3872a2486aafa9556eb0acc382d80d8932d2ad13","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/3872a2486aafa9556eb0acc382d80d8932d2ad13"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/d0bc1fcef90aa635551ee33007aab6f78b498a80","verification":{"payload":"tree 3872a2486aafa9556eb0acc382d80d8932d2ad13\nparent ad64775c77a40a1d9d117d5e5f5ef4a7dbc0b911\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1791021999 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1791021999 +0300\n\nfix(client): read `autoConnect` off the query like every other boolean (#2456)\n\nIt tested `=== \"true\"` where the rest of them test `!== \"false\"`, so a\nvalue it did not recognise turned the client off:\n\n    ?autoConnect=1   -> was off, is now left on\n    ?hmr=1           -> left on, as it always was\n    ?autoConnect=false -> off, unchanged\n\nThe default is already on, so turning it off is the only thing anyone\nwrites this option for — which makes the odd reading a trap rather than a\nstricter policy.\n\nThe existing coverage could not catch it: it only used `autoConnect=false`,\nwhich both readings treat the same way. Two tests now, one for each half,\nand the first was checked against the old reading to be sure it fails\nthere.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqwNOvCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA6C4QABlLgTdS7YwVa5GbweOlKBlw\nfiQ41XPlt3Sjrbb5P5lN8fWhJ6jab6z2SeLLVLyUCgjEzpF9qLLGmQZ7Va7pzTcz\n0WUVSIoxofwRZk3lZN/EUgqqhabR1VS0zFpeqijpRhlYMn4i6Hh80TQQyXKdpPwf\nvPglnuVG88MFwX4tFuL9EORvD1L5pu4pdfZcmgEGZ8ZM/3jmUr3r/rd4LfXYyuuw\n2r3Pt2TeOQ+PN+o3klDCZZIhsX7ux8ycpWlj/mrxv5daVcaGB1ef8qpPVlpscN+i\nUoXvMac3rynk/RVvQOxM9Z6k0jN7olc75BUdgEED+vm9tBNEeDietLhcbdLiyqNg\n1jDkT20wVO1mmxRLLsLT4I/vzilzB8gKIH1XuJkKpeErVq/taZy0zRJVIsg70INT\n7gwJ6gE6VXl1unGE5O4QB2qbc3LyeDNSEjoXs6KcO5UNNkh72khKmLfT2kKfNaQW\nIIWMxxElQvl20WyA7+imj9BMCqkjxnjyIP3gAJn3fzClxfVNQ8GBhYNdCOake+fS\nxwuq3u6eafGptbboOrCW5sf3l5D/pUc+Wp+Tsbml5bEsX/efvbtGhqcgabSYifdP\ndrxhINbQ37pX3Wj1NJZQORSyHz6ZU9YT8kSoRZ6YvT1fRr7RpSl/7N72jUq+lDQ4\nB11SFbTEhZ4gGULPKkcM\n=bpJ2\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-03T10:06:40Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/d0bc1fcef90aa635551ee33007aab6f78b498a80","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKGQwYmMxZmNlZjkwYWE2MzU1NTFlZTMzMDA3YWFiNmY3OGI0OThhODA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/ad64775c77a40a1d9d117d5e5f5ef4a7dbc0b911","sha":"ad64775c77a40a1d9d117d5e5f5ef4a7dbc0b911","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/ad64775c77a40a1d9d117d5e5f5ef4a7dbc0b911"}],"sha":"d0bc1fcef90aa635551ee33007aab6f78b498a80","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/d0bc1fcef90aa635551ee33007aab6f78b498a80"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/ad64775c77a40a1d9d117d5e5f5ef4a7dbc0b911/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-02T13:06:02Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-02T13:06:02Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat: group the cache and mime options (#2455)\n\n* feat: group the cache and mime options\n\nFour of the sixteen top-level options were the same topic and two more were\nanother, so the list read as an inbox rather than a design.\n\n    etag             -> cache.etag\n    lastModified     -> cache.lastModified\n    cacheControl     -> cache.control\n    cacheImmutable   -> cache.immutable\n    mimeTypes        -> mime.types\n    mimeTypeDefault  -> mime.default\n\n`cacheControl` and `cacheImmutable` also lose their stutter inside the\ngroup, and `mimeTypeDefault` stops existing.\n\nBoth spellings work. A legacy name warns and names its replacement, with a\nTODO to remove it at the next major. When a name is set both ways the\ngrouped one applies: the other way round, a migration that sets the new\nname and forgets to delete the old would silently not apply. That is the\nopposite of how `hot.statsOptions` resolves, which is deliberate — that\noption is a narrower legacy of `stats` rather than a rename of it.\n\nTranslation happens in one `normalizeOptions` at the entry point, so the\nseven places that read these options see one shape whichever was written.\n`NormalizedOptions` is that shape: `cache` and `mime` are always objects,\nso a read needs no guard. The legacy keys are left on the options object\nrather than deleted, since `instance.context.options` is reachable and\nsomething may still be reading them.\n\nDesign recorded in #2454, along with the proposed `build` group and the\nplan for folding `hot.client` into `hot`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: state the unit `cache.control` takes, and that folding copies\n\nAn age is given in milliseconds and sent in seconds, since `max-age` is\ndefined in seconds — so `control: 60000` sends `max-age=60`. The bullet\nlist said otherwise on two counts: `max-age=31536000000` for `true` when a\nyear in seconds is `31536000`, and `max-age=YOUR_NUMBER` for a number when\nit is divided by a thousand first. Both predate the grouping; the text came\nover verbatim when the sections moved.\n\nA table of what each input sends replaces the list, and the clamping is\nwritten down: an age above a year becomes a year, a negative one zero.\n\nAlso stated, in the changeset: `instance.context.options` is now a copy of\nthe object it was given rather than that object. Folding into the caller''s\nobject would leave `cache` populated on it, so a second middleware built\nfrom the same options would report the legacy name as conflicting with a\ngroup the caller never set — now covered by a test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* test: drive the grouped options through a response, and narrow the copy claim\n\nEvery existing cache and mime test sets the options by their flat names,\nwhich the alias folds in — so a regression that read the grouped shape\nwrongly would still have passed all of them. Three cases now go through a\nreal response on every framework: the headers `cache` asks for, the media\ntype `mime.default` falls back on, and an extension `mime.types`\nregistered.\n\nThe changeset claimed more than is true. The copy is shallow, so adding or\nreplacing a key on the object afterwards does not reach the middleware, but\nan object nested inside it still does:\n\n    top-level key added later reaches it : false\n    nested cache.control shared          : true\n    nested headers shared                : true\n\n`headers` behaves the same way and always has, so this is not particular to\n`cache.control` and nothing here changed it. Deep-copying one nested option\nand not the others would be arbitrary, and copying them in general is not\nsafe when a value can be a function or a RegExp. So the sentence says what\nthe copy actually guarantees instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3409c68d023e0019572fb7bb7b71a1dc48bf05cd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/3409c68d023e0019572fb7bb7b71a1dc48bf05cd"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/ad64775c77a40a1d9d117d5e5f5ef4a7dbc0b911","verification":{"payload":"tree 3409c68d023e0019572fb7bb7b71a1dc48bf05cd\nparent 50441b42132bb5c135da2bb386233bc65f57e240\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790946362 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790946362 +0300\n\nfeat: group the cache and mime options (#2455)\n\n* feat: group the cache and mime options\n\nFour of the sixteen top-level options were the same topic and two more were\nanother, so the list read as an inbox rather than a design.\n\n    etag             -> cache.etag\n    lastModified     -> cache.lastModified\n    cacheControl     -> cache.control\n    cacheImmutable   -> cache.immutable\n    mimeTypes        -> mime.types\n    mimeTypeDefault  -> mime.default\n\n`cacheControl` and `cacheImmutable` also lose their stutter inside the\ngroup, and `mimeTypeDefault` stops existing.\n\nBoth spellings work. A legacy name warns and names its replacement, with a\nTODO to remove it at the next major. When a name is set both ways the\ngrouped one applies: the other way round, a migration that sets the new\nname and forgets to delete the old would silently not apply. That is the\nopposite of how `hot.statsOptions` resolves, which is deliberate — that\noption is a narrower legacy of `stats` rather than a rename of it.\n\nTranslation happens in one `normalizeOptions` at the entry point, so the\nseven places that read these options see one shape whichever was written.\n`NormalizedOptions` is that shape: `cache` and `mime` are always objects,\nso a read needs no guard. The legacy keys are left on the options object\nrather than deleted, since `instance.context.options` is reachable and\nsomething may still be reading them.\n\nDesign recorded in #2454, along with the proposed `build` group and the\nplan for folding `hot.client` into `hot`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: state the unit `cache.control` takes, and that folding copies\n\nAn age is given in milliseconds and sent in seconds, since `max-age` is\ndefined in seconds — so `control: 60000` sends `max-age=60`. The bullet\nlist said otherwise on two counts: `max-age=31536000000` for `true` when a\nyear in seconds is `31536000`, and `max-age=YOUR_NUMBER` for a number when\nit is divided by a thousand first. Both predate the grouping; the text came\nover verbatim when the sections moved.\n\nA table of what each input sends replaces the list, and the clamping is\nwritten down: an age above a year becomes a year, a negative one zero.\n\nAlso stated, in the changeset: `instance.context.options` is now a copy of\nthe object it was given rather than that object. Folding into the caller''s\nobject would leave `cache` populated on it, so a second middleware built\nfrom the same options would report the legacy name as conflicting with a\ngroup the caller never set — now covered by a test.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* test: drive the grouped options through a response, and narrow the copy claim\n\nEvery existing cache and mime test sets the options by their flat names,\nwhich the alias folds in — so a regression that read the grouped shape\nwrongly would still have passed all of them. Three cases now go through a\nreal response on every framework: the headers `cache` asks for, the media\ntype `mime.default` falls back on, and an extension `mime.types`\nregistered.\n\nThe changeset claimed more than is true. The copy is shallow, so adding or\nreplacing a key on the object afterwards does not reach the middleware, but\nan object nested inside it still does:\n\n    top-level key added later reaches it : false\n    nested cache.control shared          : true\n    nested headers shared                : true\n\n`headers` behaves the same way and always has, so this is not particular to\n`cache.control` and nothing here changed it. Deep-copying one nested option\nand not the others would be arbitrary, and copying them in general is not\nsafe when a value can be a function or a RegExp. So the sentence says what\nthe copy actually guarantees instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqv6w6CRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAltIQAK0gfSWyfBLBHELITBEu7lWh\nwUm50S0IJmjaGREA5dl3mkuIZ4kKXuJVGWkgHAna4phYB11BWotASmqzxGeFKglo\nMxrwzg2TklkjOsDiL503yLscxNoiHr87F+gKa8Q259iPotFX5osnhUItxovnQwrW\nff8XCDiSbT7gnmCLHriNXz7W9GMZIYLmTxBSbRelVlopyAuA4JyS4bON0ML6gwZ0\nJggPMt3XFE/hf9qHWzN8qRwC7Gi0zkfojovlpCHWBy/WRknLH4C5vkkr7ihKawbJ\nITvg5AP/MMthNDwwBr5yHr2rTC5v3q2dBu297U9pkmkyvnhpTyOhPpBODc6KLFxD\nze00YhI94dYyHvxGwhv3vO2wLV1dihDVWlKvZRQDYs1PuoxhYoIqgD1RZ8xwuGr9\nzpng7UwimWzgJj0D1QmH4DUfptRoPtJMVLdAfCadb39TA0rsEkcqnq2Hud9BCu/5\nus7CBc/Bmu+RKSYJMDzZ3QI625/x3jZIhBCX6ODch8+yREr/9rjSOJ8G8R2lRBMg\nojhXDUfaKLYR2NBK+t4MdpdmK+SnuTlMqbXfsyi4LKZu7mVvHpFA1gQzzVp12tKF\n1q4UutGsA0/obsHJ6wcORnBjvpPTJBqMOaUhJMgP3orqKUsGp3RQA/6bLJ9H8yPf\n/4Hzso/AnHT2GilME+6e\n=Jlt/\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-02T13:06:02Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/ad64775c77a40a1d9d117d5e5f5ef4a7dbc0b911","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKGFkNjQ3NzVjNzdhNDBhMWQ5ZDExN2Q1ZTVmNWVmNGE3ZGJjMGI5MTE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/50441b42132bb5c135da2bb386233bc65f57e240","sha":"50441b42132bb5c135da2bb386233bc65f57e240","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/50441b42132bb5c135da2bb386233bc65f57e240"}],"sha":"ad64775c77a40a1d9d117d5e5f5ef4a7dbc0b911","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/ad64775c77a40a1d9d117d5e5f5ef4a7dbc0b911"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/50441b42132bb5c135da2bb386233bc65f57e240/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-02T09:36:47Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-02T09:36:47Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(hot): a secret on the endpoint, a file per transport, and `publish` (#2451)\n\n* feat(hot): require a secret on the endpoint, with `hot.token`\n\n`hot.cors` is answered by `Origin`, and that is its weakness: a browser omits\n`Origin` and the whole `Sec-Fetch-*` family when the destination is not\npotentially trustworthy — plain `http` to anything but `localhost`, which\n`host: \"0.0.0.0\"` gives you. webpack-dev-server shipped two fixes built on\nthose headers and both were bypassed exactly that way, CVE-2026-6402 and then\nCVE-2026-14620. A token asks the browser to volunteer nothing.\n\n`createHot` mints one per run, `injectHotClient` hands it to the client as\nanother entry-query option, and both wires check it with `timingSafeEqual`\nbefore anything else — so a caller without one is told nothing about which\norigins the endpoint would have allowed. A transport of your own is given it\ntoo. Driven end to end:\n\n  SSE, token: true       no token 403   wrong 403   right 200\n  WS, default            no token 403   wrong 403   right CONNECTED\n  WS, token: false       no token CONNECTED\n\nThe two transports default as they did for `cors`: `true` for the WebSocket,\nwhich is unreleased so nothing is connecting to it that would not be handed\none, and `false` for Server-Sent Events, where requiring one would refuse every\nclient already connecting.\n\nTwo things the implementation had to account for, both found by the browser\ntests rather than by reasoning:\n\n`inject: false` turns the requirement off. The token reaches the browser\nthrough the entry this middleware adds, so with nothing injected there is no\nway to hand one over and requiring it would refuse a correctly wired client.\n\nThe client needed the option after all. The first attempt folded the token into\nthe `path` query on the assumption that the client uses that verbatim — true\nfor an injected client, useless for a hand-wired entry, which has no `path`\nparameter and would not know what a bare `token=` meant. It is a client option\nlike the others now, and `hot.client.token` accepts it in node for a client\npointed at another endpoint, which keeps the two name sets identical — a test\nasserts that and caught the asymmetry.\n\nWhat it does not protect, said in the README rather than left implied: the\nclient reads the token from its entry query, so it is a string in the bundle.\nAnything that can already read the bundle cross-origin reads the token with it,\nand over plain `http` to a non-localhost address nothing stops that unless the\nserver sends `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy`. This hardens every case where the\nbundle is not readable, and is defence in depth where it is.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* refactor(hot): load only the transport that was chosen\n\n`createEventStream` moves out of `hot.js` into `src/servers/EventSourceServer.js`,\nbeside the WebSocket one, and both are required where the transport is picked\nrather than at the top of the module. Neither is reached until a `createHot`\ncall asks for it:\n\n  transport           EventSourceServer   WebSocketServer   ws\n  \"sse\" (default)     loaded              —                 —\n  \"ws\"                —                   loaded            loaded\n  your own            —                   —                 —\n\nPreviously the event stream was parsed by every consumer, including one on a\nWebSocket or on a transport of its own, because it lived in `hot.js`.\n\n`src/servers/` now holds both, which is the shape webpack-dev-server''s\n`lib/servers/` has, and the CORS and token rules sit with the transport that\nenforces them — `hot.js` keeps only the default each one starts from and the\nmint that hands a token to both.\n\n`createEventStream` is still exported from `hot.js`, as a wrapper that loads\nthe module on the first call, so an importer sees no change.\n\n`requireServer` spells each path out rather than building one from its\nargument: a bundler has to be able to see both statically.\n\nAlso fixes two fixtures that hand-wire a client and so have to carry a token of\ntheir own, which is what a developer wiring their own entry has to do: the\nworker app, and the cross-origin test that builds its own WebSocket url — that\none reads `instance.token` rather than hardcoding one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* refactor(hot): put each piece where it belongs\n\nFollow-up to extracting the event stream: the module boundaries were right but\nthree things were still on the wrong side of them.\n\nEach transport''s CORS default moves to the transport that applies it.\n`HOT_DEFAULT_CORS_SSE` and `HOT_DEFAULT_CORS_WS` lived in `utils.js`, which\n`hot.js` then imported the SSE one from purely to re-export it — it never used\nit. `resolveCors` is called inside each server, so the default each one starts\nfrom belongs there too, with the comment explaining why they differ.\n\n`pathMatch` moves to `utils.js`. `middleware.js` was reaching through `hot.js`\nfor a url helper, which is the wrong direction: the middleware does not\notherwise depend on the hot module, and every other request helper it uses is\nalready in `utils.js`. `hot.js` did not use `pathMatch` itself either — that\nimport was another re-export.\n\nAnd a comment that the lazy-loading change had stranded: \"what a transport has\nto do for itself\" describes `CLIENT_STREAM_METHODS`, and `requireServer` had\nbeen inserted between the two.\n\nWhat is left in `hot.js` is one thing: the hot lifecycle and the payloads it\npublishes — its own defaults, the contract a custom transport has to meet, and\n`createHot`. Transport mechanics are in `./servers`, request helpers in\n`./utils`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* feat(hot): add `publish`, and deprecate `hot.progress` (#2453)\n\nMeasuring a build is the server''s call rather than the middleware''s, and\n`hot.progress` made it the middleware''s: it applied `ProgressPlugin` to\nyour compiler, so a server that applies one itself — webpack-dev-server\ndoes — ends up with two of them on one compiler.\n\nWhat only the middleware can do is carry the result, and the bundled\nclient already renders `{ action: \"progress\" }`. So the option becomes one\npublic method: `instance.publish(payload)` puts a payload of your own on\nthe stream, and a server hands over what its own plugin reports. It is a\nno-op when `hot` is off, so a caller does not have to ask first, and\nnothing is sent when no client is connected — a `ProgressPlugin` tick\nfires far more often than anyone is listening.\n\n`hot.progress` keeps working until the next major release. Its browser\nend, `hot.client.progress`, is unaffected and stays: a server publishing\nits own progress payload gets it drawn with no further configuration.\n\nTwo things the option did for you become the caller''s: rounding the\npercent, and dropping a tick that rounds to the same whole number as the\nlast one.\n\nAlso fixes two README links that pointed at headings which do not exist,\nfor `etag` and for the `attach` method.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(hot): address the review on `hot.token`\n\nThree things, all consequences of the token work rather than of the\nrefactors on top of it.\n\n**A `path` that already carried a `token` ended up with two.** The client\nappended `&token=`, and the endpoint reads the first one — so the token\nconfigured here was the one ignored. A fragment was mishandled the same\nway: `path#frag` became `path#frag?token=…`, putting the query inside the\nfragment.\n\nNow in `client-src/utils/with-token.js`, where it can be tested: the url\nis taken apart on `#` and then `?` rather than parsed with `URL`, which\nwould need a base and would turn a relative path into an absolute one.\nEvery shape it can arrive in survives — absolute url, protocol-relative,\nrooted path, relative path, with or without a query or fragment.\n\n**The \"no client was injected\" warning printed the token.** Infrastructure\nwarnings travel into CI output, and a minted token is different every run,\nso printing it also invited the wrong fix — pasting a value that is\nalready stale. It names `token=<the token>` and points at the `token`\nproperty instead.\n\n**A README example read `instance.token` from a configuration that does\nnot ask for one**, so it served `{ token: false }`. It asks for one now.\nThe sentence beside it still described the per-transport defaults this\noption no longer has.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(hot): declare `hot.client.token`, and dedupe in the progress example\n\n`hot.client.token` was in the schema and serialized into the browser\nquery, but missing from the `HotClientOptions` typedef, so TypeScript\nrejected it as an excess property:\n\n    error TS2353: Object literal may only specify known properties, and\n    ''token'' does not exist in type ''HotClientOptions''.\n\nThe test that keeps the schema and the client source to one set of names\nnow covers the typedef too. That was the third place holding this list and\nthe easiest to forget, which is how the omission shipped; removing the\nline again fails the test by name.\n\nThe `publish` example published on every `ProgressPlugin` tick. The prose\nbeside it said the caller now owns both rounding the percent and dropping\na tick that repeats one, and then the example only rounded — so anyone\ncopying it sent a message per callback, most of them repeating what the\nlast one said. The example, the deprecation warning and the changeset all\nshow both now.\n\nAlso stopped overselling the no-clients short-circuit: it spares a caller\nfrom asking whether anyone is listening, but with a page open every call\nis still a message, so keeping a chatty source down to what changed stays\nthe caller''s job.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"d08a06854f0cfe40464fffed17d03c79b5128d4c","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/d08a06854f0cfe40464fffed17d03c79b5128d4c"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/50441b42132bb5c135da2bb386233bc65f57e240","verification":{"payload":"tree d08a06854f0cfe40464fffed17d03c79b5128d4c\nparent 88626e94e6e83fb610b14dae1083c03f4da25951\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790933807 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790933807 +0300\n\nfeat(hot): a secret on the endpoint, a file per transport, and `publish` (#2451)\n\n* feat(hot): require a secret on the endpoint, with `hot.token`\n\n`hot.cors` is answered by `Origin`, and that is its weakness: a browser omits\n`Origin` and the whole `Sec-Fetch-*` family when the destination is not\npotentially trustworthy — plain `http` to anything but `localhost`, which\n`host: \"0.0.0.0\"` gives you. webpack-dev-server shipped two fixes built on\nthose headers and both were bypassed exactly that way, CVE-2026-6402 and then\nCVE-2026-14620. A token asks the browser to volunteer nothing.\n\n`createHot` mints one per run, `injectHotClient` hands it to the client as\nanother entry-query option, and both wires check it with `timingSafeEqual`\nbefore anything else — so a caller without one is told nothing about which\norigins the endpoint would have allowed. A transport of your own is given it\ntoo. Driven end to end:\n\n  SSE, token: true       no token 403   wrong 403   right 200\n  WS, default            no token 403   wrong 403   right CONNECTED\n  WS, token: false       no token CONNECTED\n\nThe two transports default as they did for `cors`: `true` for the WebSocket,\nwhich is unreleased so nothing is connecting to it that would not be handed\none, and `false` for Server-Sent Events, where requiring one would refuse every\nclient already connecting.\n\nTwo things the implementation had to account for, both found by the browser\ntests rather than by reasoning:\n\n`inject: false` turns the requirement off. The token reaches the browser\nthrough the entry this middleware adds, so with nothing injected there is no\nway to hand one over and requiring it would refuse a correctly wired client.\n\nThe client needed the option after all. The first attempt folded the token into\nthe `path` query on the assumption that the client uses that verbatim — true\nfor an injected client, useless for a hand-wired entry, which has no `path`\nparameter and would not know what a bare `token=` meant. It is a client option\nlike the others now, and `hot.client.token` accepts it in node for a client\npointed at another endpoint, which keeps the two name sets identical — a test\nasserts that and caught the asymmetry.\n\nWhat it does not protect, said in the README rather than left implied: the\nclient reads the token from its entry query, so it is a string in the bundle.\nAnything that can already read the bundle cross-origin reads the token with it,\nand over plain `http` to a non-localhost address nothing stops that unless the\nserver sends `Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy`. This hardens every case where the\nbundle is not readable, and is defence in depth where it is.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* refactor(hot): load only the transport that was chosen\n\n`createEventStream` moves out of `hot.js` into `src/servers/EventSourceServer.js`,\nbeside the WebSocket one, and both are required where the transport is picked\nrather than at the top of the module. Neither is reached until a `createHot`\ncall asks for it:\n\n  transport           EventSourceServer   WebSocketServer   ws\n  \"sse\" (default)     loaded              —                 —\n  \"ws\"                —                   loaded            loaded\n  your own            —                   —                 —\n\nPreviously the event stream was parsed by every consumer, including one on a\nWebSocket or on a transport of its own, because it lived in `hot.js`.\n\n`src/servers/` now holds both, which is the shape webpack-dev-server''s\n`lib/servers/` has, and the CORS and token rules sit with the transport that\nenforces them — `hot.js` keeps only the default each one starts from and the\nmint that hands a token to both.\n\n`createEventStream` is still exported from `hot.js`, as a wrapper that loads\nthe module on the first call, so an importer sees no change.\n\n`requireServer` spells each path out rather than building one from its\nargument: a bundler has to be able to see both statically.\n\nAlso fixes two fixtures that hand-wire a client and so have to carry a token of\ntheir own, which is what a developer wiring their own entry has to do: the\nworker app, and the cross-origin test that builds its own WebSocket url — that\none reads `instance.token` rather than hardcoding one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* refactor(hot): put each piece where it belongs\n\nFollow-up to extracting the event stream: the module boundaries were right but\nthree things were still on the wrong side of them.\n\nEach transport''s CORS default moves to the transport that applies it.\n`HOT_DEFAULT_CORS_SSE` and `HOT_DEFAULT_CORS_WS` lived in `utils.js`, which\n`hot.js` then imported the SSE one from purely to re-export it — it never used\nit. `resolveCors` is called inside each server, so the default each one starts\nfrom belongs there too, with the comment explaining why they differ.\n\n`pathMatch` moves to `utils.js`. `middleware.js` was reaching through `hot.js`\nfor a url helper, which is the wrong direction: the middleware does not\notherwise depend on the hot module, and every other request helper it uses is\nalready in `utils.js`. `hot.js` did not use `pathMatch` itself either — that\nimport was another re-export.\n\nAnd a comment that the lazy-loading change had stranded: \"what a transport has\nto do for itself\" describes `CLIENT_STREAM_METHODS`, and `requireServer` had\nbeen inserted between the two.\n\nWhat is left in `hot.js` is one thing: the hot lifecycle and the payloads it\npublishes — its own defaults, the contract a custom transport has to meet, and\n`createHot`. Transport mechanics are in `./servers`, request helpers in\n`./utils`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* feat(hot): add `publish`, and deprecate `hot.progress` (#2453)\n\nMeasuring a build is the server''s call rather than the middleware''s, and\n`hot.progress` made it the middleware''s: it applied `ProgressPlugin` to\nyour compiler, so a server that applies one itself — webpack-dev-server\ndoes — ends up with two of them on one compiler.\n\nWhat only the middleware can do is carry the result, and the bundled\nclient already renders `{ action: \"progress\" }`. So the option becomes one\npublic method: `instance.publish(payload)` puts a payload of your own on\nthe stream, and a server hands over what its own plugin reports. It is a\nno-op when `hot` is off, so a caller does not have to ask first, and\nnothing is sent when no client is connected — a `ProgressPlugin` tick\nfires far more often than anyone is listening.\n\n`hot.progress` keeps working until the next major release. Its browser\nend, `hot.client.progress`, is unaffected and stays: a server publishing\nits own progress payload gets it drawn with no further configuration.\n\nTwo things the option did for you become the caller''s: rounding the\npercent, and dropping a tick that rounds to the same whole number as the\nlast one.\n\nAlso fixes two README links that pointed at headings which do not exist,\nfor `etag` and for the `attach` method.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n\n* fix(hot): address the review on `hot.token`\n\nThree things, all consequences of the token work rather than of the\nrefactors on top of it.\n\n**A `path` that already carried a `token` ended up with two.** The client\nappended `&token=`, and the endpoint reads the first one — so the token\nconfigured here was the one ignored. A fragment was mishandled the same\nway: `path#frag` became `path#frag?token=…`, putting the query inside the\nfragment.\n\nNow in `client-src/utils/with-token.js`, where it can be tested: the url\nis taken apart on `#` and then `?` rather than parsed with `URL`, which\nwould need a base and would turn a relative path into an absolute one.\nEvery shape it can arrive in survives — absolute url, protocol-relative,\nrooted path, relative path, with or without a query or fragment.\n\n**The \"no client was injected\" warning printed the token.** Infrastructure\nwarnings travel into CI output, and a minted token is different every run,\nso printing it also invited the wrong fix — pasting a value that is\nalready stale. It names `token=<the token>` and points at the `token`\nproperty instead.\n\n**A README example read `instance.token` from a configuration that does\nnot ask for one**, so it served `{ token: false }`. It asks for one now.\nThe sentence beside it still described the per-transport defaults this\noption no longer has.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(hot): declare `hot.client.token`, and dedupe in the progress example\n\n`hot.client.token` was in the schema and serialized into the browser\nquery, but missing from the `HotClientOptions` typedef, so TypeScript\nrejected it as an excess property:\n\n    error TS2353: Object literal may only specify known properties, and\n    ''token'' does not exist in type ''HotClientOptions''.\n\nThe test that keeps the schema and the client source to one set of names\nnow covers the typedef too. That was the third place holding this list and\nthe easiest to forget, which is how the omission shipped; removing the\nline again fails the test by name.\n\nThe `publish` example published on every `ProgressPlugin` tick. The prose\nbeside it said the caller now owns both rounding the percent and dropping\na tick that repeats one, and then the example only rounded — so anyone\ncopying it sent a message per callback, most of them repeating what the\nlast one said. The example, the deprecation warning and the changeset all\nshow both now.\n\nAlso stopped overselling the no-clients short-circuit: it spares a caller\nfrom asking whether anyone is listening, but with a page open every call\nis still a message, so keeping a chatty source down to what changed stays\nthe caller''s job.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqv3svCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAVbcQACXLpAXt6jFiiNo0GnUmsHJt\nLZMs9DZm5x4Kks2HQdSF5QvFi3CXc+CPup8qn9LiT4qXPiPEqZNQk/OMvmB1pMkh\nzGAPw8VJIQ3OsJOI6chGwmc4tmyXEekpHBu5KsDEw/TW/VBVRknqyH125njaY0jc\nBCoKVngdd3PzQ2MHyfv1zA5ouBrWMW3inFvELglp9OqA4BxYEgf6GesO97o4vyl0\n4KbnbnmA0VnRlDQZlVakagpjzpta4wLZB2Jk+RnOljSayk+YDz+BpVFHgnpTghmJ\n8grh//YxgsJfBmBL60QTHs97td8/fzAx6LINbCcsYig4LO3DOH2wxyv0nbCk8ESo\nUivmafgoIygFpqpdIXwD8QOL5tZ6I1GL9Yk2F/xhvHYutXxSuQGXVfjInUGEkcus\nm8XYqpUIFf3XjvwTgaLTBSZ+TS1MG6qAOWArtxdYfmJ9SV7Pi3wKtdrf1UOep6Cw\nVHdHlTWTRGACiRo2Npm/l3VEjssgKJqa6jwVU6+Av9EOLB7Zc5rjW36uLPI7e0Uk\npNdnfNS8UKnxmUeI7ZbCovjZbyqBc3EIFgc13vN3Peaqz/O8MvLiydJZSDmivOZ7\nwt2QtS+rDxgUS05ejmZjhIJnZdOV89fpdS15VvCduawbEvj7zltu+wkhRCFHldbP\nxqPuwGqDWHkn5YquDxVu\n=oR1e\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-02T09:36:48Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/50441b42132bb5c135da2bb386233bc65f57e240","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDUwNDQxYjQyMTMyYmI1YzEzNWRhMmJiMzg2MjMzYmM2NWY1N2UyNDA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/88626e94e6e83fb610b14dae1083c03f4da25951","sha":"88626e94e6e83fb610b14dae1083c03f4da25951","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/88626e94e6e83fb610b14dae1083c03f4da25951"}],"sha":"50441b42132bb5c135da2bb386233bc65f57e240","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/50441b42132bb5c135da2bb386233bc65f57e240"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/88626e94e6e83fb610b14dae1083c03f4da25951/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T23:11:24Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T23:11:24Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor: fold the small modules into `utils`, load the WebSocket server lazily (#2450)\n\n* refactor: fold the small modules into `utils`, load the WebSocket server lazily\n\n`cors.js`, `mimeTypes.js` and `injectClient.js` move into `utils.js`. All three\nwere required at the top of `index.js` or `hot.js`, so every consumer parsed\nthem on `require` either way; folding them together drops four module\nresolutions without changing a byte of what gets parsed. `utils.js` is where\nthe rest of the middleware''s helpers already live.\n\nThe conditional one goes the other way: `servers/WebSocketServer.js` is now\nrequired where a `ws` transport is built rather than at the top of `hot.js`, so\na project on the default Server-Sent Events transport never loads it.\n\n  require(\"webpack-dev-middleware\") loads\n    before   8 local modules   cors, hot, index, injectClient, middleware,\n                               mimeTypes, servers/WebSocketServer, utils\n    after    4 local modules   hot, index, middleware, utils\n\n`dist/` is a per-module build rather than a bundle, so that reduction reaches\nconsumers.\n\nOn speed, measured rather than assumed: 25 fresh processes each, median\n63.64 ms before and 63.50 ms after — a 0.14 ms difference on a require\ndominated by `schema-utils` and `memfs`. The module count is a tidiness win,\nnot a performance one. Not parsing the WebSocket server unless it is used is\nthe only real behavioural improvement here, and it is 269 lines.\n\nThree duplicate typedefs were deduplicated on the way in — `IncomingMessage`,\n`Logger` and `EXPECTED_ANY` each resolved to the same type in both files that\ndeclared them, checked before removing either.\n\nNothing public moves: `exports` names only `.` and the client paths, none of\nwhich referenced the three files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: point the editor example at where the CORS helper went\n\n`src/cors.js` is folded into `src/utils.js` in this change, and the editor\nendpoint example cited it by filename for the `Vary: Origin` precedent. Names\nthe function rather than only the file, so the next move does not strand it\nagain.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"3d319c14d074c715d176c8db9adce96e44ad4d39","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/3d319c14d074c715d176c8db9adce96e44ad4d39"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/88626e94e6e83fb610b14dae1083c03f4da25951","verification":{"payload":"tree 3d319c14d074c715d176c8db9adce96e44ad4d39\nparent ca199ccbb9e2d25432b58ccb4f671765732ee831\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790896284 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790896284 +0300\n\nrefactor: fold the small modules into `utils`, load the WebSocket server lazily (#2450)\n\n* refactor: fold the small modules into `utils`, load the WebSocket server lazily\n\n`cors.js`, `mimeTypes.js` and `injectClient.js` move into `utils.js`. All three\nwere required at the top of `index.js` or `hot.js`, so every consumer parsed\nthem on `require` either way; folding them together drops four module\nresolutions without changing a byte of what gets parsed. `utils.js` is where\nthe rest of the middleware''s helpers already live.\n\nThe conditional one goes the other way: `servers/WebSocketServer.js` is now\nrequired where a `ws` transport is built rather than at the top of `hot.js`, so\na project on the default Server-Sent Events transport never loads it.\n\n  require(\"webpack-dev-middleware\") loads\n    before   8 local modules   cors, hot, index, injectClient, middleware,\n                               mimeTypes, servers/WebSocketServer, utils\n    after    4 local modules   hot, index, middleware, utils\n\n`dist/` is a per-module build rather than a bundle, so that reduction reaches\nconsumers.\n\nOn speed, measured rather than assumed: 25 fresh processes each, median\n63.64 ms before and 63.50 ms after — a 0.14 ms difference on a require\ndominated by `schema-utils` and `memfs`. The module count is a tidiness win,\nnot a performance one. Not parsing the WebSocket server unless it is used is\nthe only real behavioural improvement here, and it is 269 lines.\n\nThree duplicate typedefs were deduplicated on the way in — `IncomingMessage`,\n`Logger` and `EXPECTED_ANY` each resolved to the same type in both files that\ndeclared them, checked before removing either.\n\nNothing public moves: `exports` names only `.` and the client paths, none of\nwhich referenced the three files.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: point the editor example at where the CORS helper went\n\n`src/cors.js` is folded into `src/utils.js` in this change, and the editor\nendpoint example cited it by filename for the `Vary: Origin` precedent. Names\nthe function rather than only the file, so the next move does not strand it\nagain.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvuicCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA4PAQABWKV8rT4uvbz1ANmXOM2LGY\nRHT0cwyzNRg3mD9gm70xedkhNoI+iork/d6+3LBkJxd3umw4aH1b12jaJHXF/N00\nipqR6d8bkH99hRlEHQafSBTu2EaSTA0nE3iYyFBDhho3G2XHp/K1kb6anvtrXOa6\nW5WlF5r7EhhC0T7iiOtPrD0ZcZ/49MxOBImzzgGn+my7c139B7jH7OfV4rEFuJos\npnehDrqhfbi9by/t1tkzvHBPDgcowEcXnATsNr68FGL62RfCMSSrtvMJVLmzIGdb\n3tkSLrQtstgJ6u7gFcw0ZgEbh4EO/h09FYqxQtDDjdMErYL/nBJtXLHkG5g8N17Z\nKW3wGSDdwsDDlFDUW3iViJC1VZZo6o0PkUR2ymYlAjFw+zF8x1HfLkbbZKw3vijj\nAvT1plfHlFnxMl4cTtQWFI67PzzpSprMOjjW2PYeCio+1piHh2NHo6GoaxCt7bjU\n5EzyBEZg2blInFpLgC1hBEkMV/0fy1j+92/ZycNkXxTPzYO+miMew1yFhB8DvjjO\neNgtYhRbx594UYFkHGAAfLR/W0jiyED21dEbZi1UjgTDlrR1K29A3/rzP192nj8A\ncxIO0liWyeiKAgsci2HWvZEqfqFX9ZoweLoEUEi8d6hzpVS2Nd+B21YaNIrLiffT\nrx6bLUs3+11b2fFjgnna\n=2pql\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-01T23:11:25Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/88626e94e6e83fb610b14dae1083c03f4da25951","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDg4NjI2ZTk0ZTZlODNmYjYxMGIxNGRhZTEwODNjMDNmNGRhMjU5NTE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/ca199ccbb9e2d25432b58ccb4f671765732ee831","sha":"ca199ccbb9e2d25432b58ccb4f671765732ee831","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/ca199ccbb9e2d25432b58ccb4f671765732ee831"}],"sha":"88626e94e6e83fb610b14dae1083c03f4da25951","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/88626e94e6e83fb610b14dae1083c03f4da25951"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/ca199ccbb9e2d25432b58ccb4f671765732ee831/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T20:36:41Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T20:36:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"docs: say that an editor endpoint has to check the path it is handed (#2448)\n\n* docs: say that an editor endpoint has to check the path it is handed\n\nThe path the overlay sends to `openEditorEndpoint` is cut out of the build''s\nerror text, and that text is written by loaders and dependencies rather than\nby the project. An absolute path with an extension in it becomes a chip like\nany other: an error carrying `/root/.ssh/id_rsa.pub:1:1` offers a developer\nsomething to click that asks their endpoint to open it.\n\nVerified against the pattern that makes the chips — `/etc/passwd` is not\nmatched, having no extension, but `/root/.ssh/id_rsa.pub:1:1` is.\n\nNobody reads the file back and the reference is visible before it is clicked,\nso this is a small thing. But the endpoint is the only place that knows where\nthe project is, which makes the check its job, and nothing said so. The\nSecurity section now does, with an example that resolves the path and\ncompares it with `path.relative` — `startsWith` would let a sibling of the\nroot through, the same way it would have in `getFilenameFromUrl`.\n\nThe position is stripped off the end rather than at the first colon, because\na Windows path opens with a drive letter and a colon of its own, and\n`C:/Users/me/a.js:1:1` does match the pattern. Every case in that example was\nrun: a project-relative path opens, a home-directory path, a `..` path, a\nsibling-of-the-root path and an empty one are refused, and a drive path\nresolves the way the platform means it to.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: canonicalize the path before the editor endpoint accepts it\n\n`path.resolve` and `path.relative` compare the names they are given, not\nwhere those names lead, so a symlink inside the project passes a containment\ncheck and the editor opens whatever it points at. A package can ship one and\n`node_modules` sits inside the root, which is enough of a chain to matter in\nan example people will copy.\n\nDemonstrated before changing it: a link at\n`node_modules/evil/link.js -> /tmp/outside-secret.key`, named by an error\nmessage, was accepted by the previous example and is refused by this one.\n\n`realpath` on the root as well, or every file under a symlinked root is\nrefused — which `/tmp` is on macOS, verified. A reference to a file that was\nnever there now answers `404` rather than reaching the editor, and the `..`\ntest is the exact form `getFilenameFromUrl` uses, so a file named `..foo.js`\nis not refused for its name.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: check who asked the editor endpoint, not only which file\n\nThe path checks say which file may be opened; they do not say who may ask.\nOpening an editor is a side effect and nothing is read back, so any page the\ndeveloper happens to be visiting can trigger it with a bare `<img>` pointed at\nthe endpoint — no CORS, no fetch, nothing for the browser to refuse.\n\n`Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site` is what that request carries and what the overlay\nnever sends: its own `fetch`, from inside the iframe it builds, reads\n`same-origin` on the app''s own origin and `same-site` when the endpoint is on\nanother port. Measured in Chromium both ways rather than taken from the spec.\n\nAlso notes the one case that has to replace the check rather than keep it: an\nendpoint on a genuinely different site, where the overlay is cross-site too, so\nthe `Origin` has to be compared against an allowlist instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: require the editor endpoint''s caller to be same-origin\n\nRefusing `cross-site` was one attack shape out of several. Measured in\nChromium, across every way the endpoint can be reached:\n\n  overlay, endpoint on same origin   Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin  Origin: -\n  overlay, endpoint on another port  Sec-Fetch-Site: same-site    Origin: set\n  overlay over plain-http LAN        Sec-Fetch-Site: -            Origin: -\n  img from another port              Sec-Fetch-Site: same-site    Origin: -\n  img from another host              Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site   Origin: -\n  img, plain-http LAN target         Sec-Fetch-Site: -            Origin: -\n\nOnly the legitimate request says `same-origin`, so that is the value to\nrequire — a page on another port of the same host reads `same-site` and went\nstraight through the old check.\n\nTwo residues are now stated rather than left implied. An endpoint deliberately\non another origin cannot use this check at all, and gets the `Origin`\nallowlist, which is equivalent only because it requires the header to be\npresent: a cross-origin `fetch` always sends one and an `img` or `form` cannot.\nAnd over plain `http` to a non-loopback address — `host: \"0.0.0.0\"`, opened\nfrom a phone — browsers send no Fetch Metadata and no `Origin` on either the\noverlay''s request or an attack, so nothing distinguishes them; the guard\nrefuses everything there, which is the safe direction but worth knowing.\n\nAlso notes that binding the route to loopback does not help by itself, since\nthe browser running the attacker''s page is on the developer''s machine.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: make the cross-origin editor check a swap, not a second route\n\nThe alternative was written as another `app.get` on the same path, calling a\n`resolveAndOpen` that does not exist — a placeholder added to satisfy a lint\nrule, which made the snippet unrunnable and implied a route that could never be\nreached anyway, since the handler above it answers and never calls `next`.\n\nThe caller check is a named predicate now, so the cross-origin case is the one\ndefinition to replace and the route and its path checks stay as they are.\n\nBoth documented shapes run, and answer the same way:\n\n  the overlay                   opened .../src/app.js\n  a page on another port        REFUSED 403 Not an allowed caller\n  a page on another host        REFUSED 403 Not an allowed caller\n  an img or form (no headers)   REFUSED 403 Not an allowed caller\n  symlink out of the project    REFUSED 403 Outside the project\n  traversal                     REFUSED 403 Outside the project\n  a file named ..foo            opened .../src/..foo.js\n  never existed                 REFUSED 404 No such file\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: answer the overlay''s cross-origin fetch, in that variant only\n\nThe `Origin` allowlist decided who may ask, but the cross-origin variant never\nanswered with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, so the browser discarded the\nresponse the overlay reads. Driven from Chromium, that is the worst of both:\n\n  without the header: the overlay saw a failure — Failed to fetch\n  with the header   : the overlay saw ok\n\n  the editor was asked to open a file: without the header, with the header\n\nThe file opens either way, because the request reached the server — and the\noverlay still logs that it could not open it.\n\nThe header is set in the swapped definition, after the allowlist has already\nrefused every other origin and every request carrying none, and not in the\nshared route, where the default predicate can accept a request that has no\n`Origin` to echo. One consequence worth having: because the header is set\nbefore the path checks run, the overlay can read a genuine 403 or 404 from\nthem instead of a blocked response it cannot tell apart from a dead server.\n\nDriven by a real browser, end to end:\n\n  the overlay, allowlisted origin -> the overlay saw ok\n  symlink out of the project      -> the overlay saw status 403\n  traversal                       -> the overlay saw status 403\n  a file named ..foo              -> the overlay saw ok\n  a page on another origin        -> the overlay saw a failure: Failed to fetch\n\n  the editor was asked to open: .../src/app.js, .../src/..foo.js\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: keep the editor endpoint''s answers out of caches\n\nThe cross-origin variant echoed the request''s `Origin` into\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin` with no `Vary`, so a cache holding one allowed\norigin''s response could hand it to another allowed origin, grant and all —\nwhich `fetch` then rejects, and the overlay reports as a failure. The\nmiddleware''s own hot endpoint already gets this right in `src/cors.js:165`, for\nthe same reason; the example did not.\n\n`Vary: Origin` is set before the allowlist check, so the refusal is keyed by\norigin too, and `Cache-Control: no-store` goes on the route itself. The second\nis the one that covers the default variant as well: its answer depends on\n`Sec-Fetch-Site` rather than `Origin`, and an endpoint that exists for a side\neffect and returns no body has nothing worth storing under any key.\n\nDriven against both documented shapes:\n\n  default\n    the overlay              200  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: -       ACAO: -\n    another port             403  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: -       ACAO: -\n    no Fetch Metadata        403  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: -       ACAO: -\n\n  cross-origin swap\n    allowlisted origin       200  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: Origin  ACAO: http://localhost:8080\n    another origin           403  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: Origin  ACAO: -\n    no Origin (img or form)  403  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: Origin  ACAO: -\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"6073a765192218b81f041669d8db75607d045e58","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/6073a765192218b81f041669d8db75607d045e58"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/ca199ccbb9e2d25432b58ccb4f671765732ee831","verification":{"payload":"tree 6073a765192218b81f041669d8db75607d045e58\nparent 90b72bce7e5e55f90a1f1753c193b6533514720f\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790887001 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790887001 +0300\n\ndocs: say that an editor endpoint has to check the path it is handed (#2448)\n\n* docs: say that an editor endpoint has to check the path it is handed\n\nThe path the overlay sends to `openEditorEndpoint` is cut out of the build''s\nerror text, and that text is written by loaders and dependencies rather than\nby the project. An absolute path with an extension in it becomes a chip like\nany other: an error carrying `/root/.ssh/id_rsa.pub:1:1` offers a developer\nsomething to click that asks their endpoint to open it.\n\nVerified against the pattern that makes the chips — `/etc/passwd` is not\nmatched, having no extension, but `/root/.ssh/id_rsa.pub:1:1` is.\n\nNobody reads the file back and the reference is visible before it is clicked,\nso this is a small thing. But the endpoint is the only place that knows where\nthe project is, which makes the check its job, and nothing said so. The\nSecurity section now does, with an example that resolves the path and\ncompares it with `path.relative` — `startsWith` would let a sibling of the\nroot through, the same way it would have in `getFilenameFromUrl`.\n\nThe position is stripped off the end rather than at the first colon, because\na Windows path opens with a drive letter and a colon of its own, and\n`C:/Users/me/a.js:1:1` does match the pattern. Every case in that example was\nrun: a project-relative path opens, a home-directory path, a `..` path, a\nsibling-of-the-root path and an empty one are refused, and a drive path\nresolves the way the platform means it to.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: canonicalize the path before the editor endpoint accepts it\n\n`path.resolve` and `path.relative` compare the names they are given, not\nwhere those names lead, so a symlink inside the project passes a containment\ncheck and the editor opens whatever it points at. A package can ship one and\n`node_modules` sits inside the root, which is enough of a chain to matter in\nan example people will copy.\n\nDemonstrated before changing it: a link at\n`node_modules/evil/link.js -> /tmp/outside-secret.key`, named by an error\nmessage, was accepted by the previous example and is refused by this one.\n\n`realpath` on the root as well, or every file under a symlinked root is\nrefused — which `/tmp` is on macOS, verified. A reference to a file that was\nnever there now answers `404` rather than reaching the editor, and the `..`\ntest is the exact form `getFilenameFromUrl` uses, so a file named `..foo.js`\nis not refused for its name.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: check who asked the editor endpoint, not only which file\n\nThe path checks say which file may be opened; they do not say who may ask.\nOpening an editor is a side effect and nothing is read back, so any page the\ndeveloper happens to be visiting can trigger it with a bare `<img>` pointed at\nthe endpoint — no CORS, no fetch, nothing for the browser to refuse.\n\n`Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site` is what that request carries and what the overlay\nnever sends: its own `fetch`, from inside the iframe it builds, reads\n`same-origin` on the app''s own origin and `same-site` when the endpoint is on\nanother port. Measured in Chromium both ways rather than taken from the spec.\n\nAlso notes the one case that has to replace the check rather than keep it: an\nendpoint on a genuinely different site, where the overlay is cross-site too, so\nthe `Origin` has to be compared against an allowlist instead.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: require the editor endpoint''s caller to be same-origin\n\nRefusing `cross-site` was one attack shape out of several. Measured in\nChromium, across every way the endpoint can be reached:\n\n  overlay, endpoint on same origin   Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin  Origin: -\n  overlay, endpoint on another port  Sec-Fetch-Site: same-site    Origin: set\n  overlay over plain-http LAN        Sec-Fetch-Site: -            Origin: -\n  img from another port              Sec-Fetch-Site: same-site    Origin: -\n  img from another host              Sec-Fetch-Site: cross-site   Origin: -\n  img, plain-http LAN target         Sec-Fetch-Site: -            Origin: -\n\nOnly the legitimate request says `same-origin`, so that is the value to\nrequire — a page on another port of the same host reads `same-site` and went\nstraight through the old check.\n\nTwo residues are now stated rather than left implied. An endpoint deliberately\non another origin cannot use this check at all, and gets the `Origin`\nallowlist, which is equivalent only because it requires the header to be\npresent: a cross-origin `fetch` always sends one and an `img` or `form` cannot.\nAnd over plain `http` to a non-loopback address — `host: \"0.0.0.0\"`, opened\nfrom a phone — browsers send no Fetch Metadata and no `Origin` on either the\noverlay''s request or an attack, so nothing distinguishes them; the guard\nrefuses everything there, which is the safe direction but worth knowing.\n\nAlso notes that binding the route to loopback does not help by itself, since\nthe browser running the attacker''s page is on the developer''s machine.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: make the cross-origin editor check a swap, not a second route\n\nThe alternative was written as another `app.get` on the same path, calling a\n`resolveAndOpen` that does not exist — a placeholder added to satisfy a lint\nrule, which made the snippet unrunnable and implied a route that could never be\nreached anyway, since the handler above it answers and never calls `next`.\n\nThe caller check is a named predicate now, so the cross-origin case is the one\ndefinition to replace and the route and its path checks stay as they are.\n\nBoth documented shapes run, and answer the same way:\n\n  the overlay                   opened .../src/app.js\n  a page on another port        REFUSED 403 Not an allowed caller\n  a page on another host        REFUSED 403 Not an allowed caller\n  an img or form (no headers)   REFUSED 403 Not an allowed caller\n  symlink out of the project    REFUSED 403 Outside the project\n  traversal                     REFUSED 403 Outside the project\n  a file named ..foo            opened .../src/..foo.js\n  never existed                 REFUSED 404 No such file\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: answer the overlay''s cross-origin fetch, in that variant only\n\nThe `Origin` allowlist decided who may ask, but the cross-origin variant never\nanswered with `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`, so the browser discarded the\nresponse the overlay reads. Driven from Chromium, that is the worst of both:\n\n  without the header: the overlay saw a failure — Failed to fetch\n  with the header   : the overlay saw ok\n\n  the editor was asked to open a file: without the header, with the header\n\nThe file opens either way, because the request reached the server — and the\noverlay still logs that it could not open it.\n\nThe header is set in the swapped definition, after the allowlist has already\nrefused every other origin and every request carrying none, and not in the\nshared route, where the default predicate can accept a request that has no\n`Origin` to echo. One consequence worth having: because the header is set\nbefore the path checks run, the overlay can read a genuine 403 or 404 from\nthem instead of a blocked response it cannot tell apart from a dead server.\n\nDriven by a real browser, end to end:\n\n  the overlay, allowlisted origin -> the overlay saw ok\n  symlink out of the project      -> the overlay saw status 403\n  traversal                       -> the overlay saw status 403\n  a file named ..foo              -> the overlay saw ok\n  a page on another origin        -> the overlay saw a failure: Failed to fetch\n\n  the editor was asked to open: .../src/app.js, .../src/..foo.js\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: keep the editor endpoint''s answers out of caches\n\nThe cross-origin variant echoed the request''s `Origin` into\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin` with no `Vary`, so a cache holding one allowed\norigin''s response could hand it to another allowed origin, grant and all —\nwhich `fetch` then rejects, and the overlay reports as a failure. The\nmiddleware''s own hot endpoint already gets this right in `src/cors.js:165`, for\nthe same reason; the example did not.\n\n`Vary: Origin` is set before the allowlist check, so the refusal is keyed by\norigin too, and `Cache-Control: no-store` goes on the route itself. The second\nis the one that covers the default variant as well: its answer depends on\n`Sec-Fetch-Site` rather than `Origin`, and an endpoint that exists for a side\neffect and returns no body has nothing worth storing under any key.\n\nDriven against both documented shapes:\n\n  default\n    the overlay              200  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: -       ACAO: -\n    another port             403  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: -       ACAO: -\n    no Fetch Metadata        403  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: -       ACAO: -\n\n  cross-origin swap\n    allowlisted origin       200  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: Origin  ACAO: http://localhost:8080\n    another origin           403  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: Origin  ACAO: -\n    no Origin (img or form)  403  Cache-Control: no-store  Vary: Origin  ACAO: -\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvsRZCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAANp0QAHI84oCr03I3Ht9ywWpKQu6L\nU1xfi/T337WKR+4R5sb8jpOOzBYK2aWziH9bGxsCdPtt9P5lSselIv6N1YRMBdky\n7NtfGg+ZnL2TZKfGcqyuGnIIimOfe0oXfl2cq1kpe6sXWg22dLacYJDx/BnEdx9U\nbzZIEpFQAwme6mkh2tBWvjur6AsaJQqQVJ0rWLCTyjKT63ls9lAXgZ0NYerTWlfK\nUg+XnOguugzNYB+OZ+1ugc8b2g+ZygPIxE3cZjj1i6clY/LeGkxw8ecejuzGaK1j\ndpXgjR4Qh9Mq5kAtQH8RtCLhSHNMEdxwrxFwzyAkH6WzWsuX0vee27QZk68nDaMX\n5lC4Cxt/5BRcPOOla3Et7DcxbkqwU1Bg1mVJFsmIPZXTz08ZKsizmktEqtbVMn1o\n5Yxn6UjERJTC8kBwu6krVtS8A4/U5dHevi09hGkM29YdMNWDVqO29aRKZ1YJZ/GU\nTJDl3M/kwKeJE1Grtoy2le5s3OS+AKFEmRWvvoAsoA8QoLMIJI7duWeCg6XdEng1\nWk6ddenKWIKv4OfSSAJd2qEH0GQwpM0KCvvaX6nGGdLi411Jo1AdeEp75WBzZNQU\n4ZQLUnHrY7u3a3QKMClE0G8EIazZ9TN+N3ogUWB+GIB+sfclofNjbgl07OYYFdpU\nhoUOwInpeNqI1Ot2Pj0U\n=QPbL\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-01T20:36:42Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/ca199ccbb9e2d25432b58ccb4f671765732ee831","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKGNhMTk5Y2NiYjllMmQyNTQzMmI1OGNjYjRmNjcxNzY1NzMyZWU4MzE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/90b72bce7e5e55f90a1f1753c193b6533514720f","sha":"90b72bce7e5e55f90a1f1753c193b6533514720f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/90b72bce7e5e55f90a1f1753c193b6533514720f"}],"sha":"ca199ccbb9e2d25432b58ccb4f671765732ee831","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/ca199ccbb9e2d25432b58ccb4f671765732ee831"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/90b72bce7e5e55f90a1f1753c193b6533514720f/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T19:28:17Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T19:28:17Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor: resolve media types through `mime-db` directly (#2449)\n\n* refactor: resolve media types through `mime-db` directly\n\n`mime-types` is `mime-db` plus an extension table and a scoring rule, and\nwebpack already depends on `mime-db` and scores it the same way — so going\nthrough `mime-types` added a package to every install and a second version\nrange over the same data. The table and the scoring move here, ported from\n`jshttp/mime-types`, and `test/mimeTypes.test.js` holds the result to\n`mime-types` over every extension in `mime-db` for `lookup`, `contentType`\nand `charset`, so the data can only change by a diff that says so.\n\nThe table is per instance now, which the `mimeTypes` option always meant: it\nused to be applied by assigning to the table `mime-types` exports, one object\nshared by every consumer in the process, so two middlewares accumulated into\na single map and anything else requiring `mime-types` inherited whatever a\nmiddleware had registered. The option is read ahead of the known extensions\ninstead.\n\n`mime-db` is required on the first type lookup rather than at load, so a run\nthat never answers an HTTP request does not pay to parse a megabyte of JSON.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* refactor: read the `mimeTypes` option once, as it was read before\n\nThe option was spread into a table at construction, so the object a caller\npassed stopped mattering the moment the middleware was built. Reading it on\neach lookup quietly changed that — a later mutation took effect, and two\ninstances handed the same object could reach each other through it:\n\n  before   after mutating the same object: text/first   / false\n  live     after mutating the same object: text/mutated / text/added-later\n\nCopied at construction instead, which restores both. The copy has a null\nprototype, so the `hasOwn` guard that kept `constructor` and `toString` from\nresolving as extensions is no longer needed to get that.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"eeadef3f054b73ab2d0c41510207721fd02c23d9","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/eeadef3f054b73ab2d0c41510207721fd02c23d9"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/90b72bce7e5e55f90a1f1753c193b6533514720f","verification":{"payload":"tree eeadef3f054b73ab2d0c41510207721fd02c23d9\nparent da18d0790b9972972c4557eb29a83552940f0150\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790882897 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790882897 +0300\n\nrefactor: resolve media types through `mime-db` directly (#2449)\n\n* refactor: resolve media types through `mime-db` directly\n\n`mime-types` is `mime-db` plus an extension table and a scoring rule, and\nwebpack already depends on `mime-db` and scores it the same way — so going\nthrough `mime-types` added a package to every install and a second version\nrange over the same data. The table and the scoring move here, ported from\n`jshttp/mime-types`, and `test/mimeTypes.test.js` holds the result to\n`mime-types` over every extension in `mime-db` for `lookup`, `contentType`\nand `charset`, so the data can only change by a diff that says so.\n\nThe table is per instance now, which the `mimeTypes` option always meant: it\nused to be applied by assigning to the table `mime-types` exports, one object\nshared by every consumer in the process, so two middlewares accumulated into\na single map and anything else requiring `mime-types` inherited whatever a\nmiddleware had registered. The option is read ahead of the known extensions\ninstead.\n\n`mime-db` is required on the first type lookup rather than at load, so a run\nthat never answers an HTTP request does not pay to parse a megabyte of JSON.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* refactor: read the `mimeTypes` option once, as it was read before\n\nThe option was spread into a table at construction, so the object a caller\npassed stopped mattering the moment the middleware was built. Reading it on\neach lookup quietly changed that — a later mutation took effect, and two\ninstances handed the same object could reach each other through it:\n\n  before   after mutating the same object: text/first   / false\n  live     after mutating the same object: text/mutated / text/added-later\n\nCopied at construction instead, which restores both. The copy has a null\nprototype, so the `hasOwn` guard that kept `constructor` and `toString` from\nresolving as extensions is no longer needed to get that.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvrRRCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAsYUQAJcBulHWno0qu0PEbO2N1+KN\nVstwZ/SSiaTVpkCRboIhO5gMXLyFwpVT9hdSD5FzipudDE7rm9DxLEDAc3YPUYNy\nsyQMbReCK7yd5a293VRKD1owIbVxFYr42DEW/hlvIUHZ6+Q4VTCpA1J4HWWJazEE\nyWZ98HEIA+KdjDM2DQtnkOo/EA6luFaCjUCj2sl2QL8vt4/wvjT/S7++SBSusW3x\nNhlzn5ARNUrpNtoD7+dMG/bxJ0uFHmYojW9hwFHSEi5yu5uVstDFCA80aKxnLKC0\njDlg/YLQebRnwsymbdGmznMerBrUS+XXnKnPOcX0a6uuvvl9NNsTxc+/MrPZcjG/\n3XPUa59bnA0VIQvrsgAnomWOoSyxEkOhQKJAR5H1Mys31qkzESAB75tvV4bTex9m\nxtFUR0QSOMZ6zqBcF8ebOBvzyhbqkvqmfJi0yS5BHYEM8OGD0HUGM+0JlB++X5Cz\noAAmhJkW9rwRhjZ0R6cZwUWTIpG3RkVDJJfDB1ABdiqP+UbF5uperfJZeyYkXfd+\n0mlOo37QT8fUXiLkqda316UY1LdJ6B3iybxzqUJUxzP/eaGa4yePg7u+7q/MPCKY\npa0zyPpuSFFJqQevARZ8hj5ko6H0drpNJWobHfid/URxbh6013xMXcbrakioxPfO\nd0wWuZ88YCVd0sYEMN3F\n=0Ama\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-01T19:28:18Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/90b72bce7e5e55f90a1f1753c193b6533514720f","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDkwYjcyYmNlN2U1ZTU1ZjkwYTFmMTc1M2MxOTNiNjUzMzUxNDcyMGY","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/da18d0790b9972972c4557eb29a83552940f0150","sha":"da18d0790b9972972c4557eb29a83552940f0150","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/da18d0790b9972972c4557eb29a83552940f0150"}],"sha":"90b72bce7e5e55f90a1f1753c193b6533514720f","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/90b72bce7e5e55f90a1f1753c193b6533514720f"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/da18d0790b9972972c4557eb29a83552940f0150/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T14:02:40Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T14:02:40Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"test(e2e): hold the overlay to rendering what it is given, plus CodeQL (#2446)\n\n* test(e2e): hold the overlay to rendering what it is given, not running it\n\nA build''s output reaches the overlay as HTML, and none of the text in it is\nours: a module''s own source turns up in a parse error''s code frame, loaders\nand plugins write their own messages, and file paths come from the\nfilesystem. Between the escape and the card there are four passes that insert\nmarkup of their own — ANSI colours, the code-frame highlighter, the file\nchips, the url linkifier — and each one is a chance to put a payload back\ntogether. Nothing was holding that shut but reading the code in the right\norder.\n\nEleven payloads, each compiled into a module that does not parse, asserted\ninert in a real browser: no global set in the page or in the overlay''s own\ndocument, no script or iframe element, no attribute starting with `on`, no\n`javascript:` href. They cover the plain injections, one arriving through an\nANSI sequence, two shaped to break out of the linkifier''s `href`, one shaped\nto break out of a file chip''s `style`, and one closing a span the highlighters\nopened.\n\nNeutering the escaper alone fires eight of the eleven, so the suite is\nmeasuring something. The remaining three are payloads that were never going\nto execute on their own; they stay because what makes them harmless is the\nsame escaping.\n\nAdded CodeQL, which is the part the tests cannot do: they check the cases\nsomebody thought of, and it reads every path for the shapes that have bitten\nthis package before — markup built from a build''s output, a filename built\nfrom a request, a regular expression run on either. `security-extended`\nrather than the default suite, because those four queries are in the extended\none. Analysis is scoped to the sources; `dist/`, `client/` and `types/` are\ngenerated from them, so reading those reports the same finding twice against\na file nobody edits, and the fixtures are hostile on purpose.\n\nExpect the overlay''s `innerHTML` to be reported on the first run. It is a\ndeliberate sink, behind the escaping these tests now cover and behind a\nTrusted Types policy where a page enforces one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* test(e2e): make the worker suite say why it failed, and retry while it does\n\nThe worker suite fails roughly one run in ten: an update never reaches the\nworker''s module and the test waits out a sixty-second timeout that says only\nthat two messages did not arrive. It is what turned this branch red, and it\nhas nothing to do with what the branch changes.\n\nNarrowed as far as it goes without instrumenting the client:\n\n  * `settle()` holds the page until the watcher is quiet, so the bundle it\n    asks for is not from a build about to be superseded — the shared harness\n    has carried this guard for the same reason, and this suite had its own\n    copy of that harness without it;\n  * `nextBuild()`, registered before the edit and awaited after it, proves\n    the watcher saw the change and the rebuild finished;\n  * the timeout drops to thirty seconds and the failure now reports what the\n    worker did send, plus where to look for the one failure that presents as\n    silence — the client cannot apply an update, falls back to a reload a\n    worker cannot do to itself, says so once and waits.\n\nWith that in place a failing run shows the rebuild completing, the worker\nhaving sent only `v1`, and the worker''s console carrying no complaint from\nthe client. So it is not the watcher, not a stale hash, and not a failed\napply; the module simply does not re-run. Holding the edit until `onConnect`\nhad fired made no difference either, and is not kept — the bare case has no\nclient of its own, so waiting for one hangs rather than fails.\n\nThat leaves `process-update.js`, which wants the worker''s console relayed out\nto go further, and the next person should not pay for this twice: the TODO\nlists what has been eliminated. Until then `jest.retryTimes(3, {\nlogErrorsBeforeRetry: true })`, the same way the framework matrix handles its\nown upstream flake — retried, not skipped, and still logged, so it cannot\nquietly become normal.\n\nVerified on an idle machine: worker, escaping and overlay together, 72 tests,\nno retry needed. A full `test/e2e` run was attempted and abandoned — repeated\nprobe loops had left orphaned browsers behind, and unrelated suites were\ntiming out on a starved container rather than on anything in the code.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"79da7d56081314b533de4213c9e3aa91ad409112","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/79da7d56081314b533de4213c9e3aa91ad409112"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/da18d0790b9972972c4557eb29a83552940f0150","verification":{"payload":"tree 79da7d56081314b533de4213c9e3aa91ad409112\nparent 22d2b7b81db4e9c2fbc3db238aa5c17f85df09c4\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790863360 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790863360 +0300\n\ntest(e2e): hold the overlay to rendering what it is given, plus CodeQL (#2446)\n\n* test(e2e): hold the overlay to rendering what it is given, not running it\n\nA build''s output reaches the overlay as HTML, and none of the text in it is\nours: a module''s own source turns up in a parse error''s code frame, loaders\nand plugins write their own messages, and file paths come from the\nfilesystem. Between the escape and the card there are four passes that insert\nmarkup of their own — ANSI colours, the code-frame highlighter, the file\nchips, the url linkifier — and each one is a chance to put a payload back\ntogether. Nothing was holding that shut but reading the code in the right\norder.\n\nEleven payloads, each compiled into a module that does not parse, asserted\ninert in a real browser: no global set in the page or in the overlay''s own\ndocument, no script or iframe element, no attribute starting with `on`, no\n`javascript:` href. They cover the plain injections, one arriving through an\nANSI sequence, two shaped to break out of the linkifier''s `href`, one shaped\nto break out of a file chip''s `style`, and one closing a span the highlighters\nopened.\n\nNeutering the escaper alone fires eight of the eleven, so the suite is\nmeasuring something. The remaining three are payloads that were never going\nto execute on their own; they stay because what makes them harmless is the\nsame escaping.\n\nAdded CodeQL, which is the part the tests cannot do: they check the cases\nsomebody thought of, and it reads every path for the shapes that have bitten\nthis package before — markup built from a build''s output, a filename built\nfrom a request, a regular expression run on either. `security-extended`\nrather than the default suite, because those four queries are in the extended\none. Analysis is scoped to the sources; `dist/`, `client/` and `types/` are\ngenerated from them, so reading those reports the same finding twice against\na file nobody edits, and the fixtures are hostile on purpose.\n\nExpect the overlay''s `innerHTML` to be reported on the first run. It is a\ndeliberate sink, behind the escaping these tests now cover and behind a\nTrusted Types policy where a page enforces one.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* test(e2e): make the worker suite say why it failed, and retry while it does\n\nThe worker suite fails roughly one run in ten: an update never reaches the\nworker''s module and the test waits out a sixty-second timeout that says only\nthat two messages did not arrive. It is what turned this branch red, and it\nhas nothing to do with what the branch changes.\n\nNarrowed as far as it goes without instrumenting the client:\n\n  * `settle()` holds the page until the watcher is quiet, so the bundle it\n    asks for is not from a build about to be superseded — the shared harness\n    has carried this guard for the same reason, and this suite had its own\n    copy of that harness without it;\n  * `nextBuild()`, registered before the edit and awaited after it, proves\n    the watcher saw the change and the rebuild finished;\n  * the timeout drops to thirty seconds and the failure now reports what the\n    worker did send, plus where to look for the one failure that presents as\n    silence — the client cannot apply an update, falls back to a reload a\n    worker cannot do to itself, says so once and waits.\n\nWith that in place a failing run shows the rebuild completing, the worker\nhaving sent only `v1`, and the worker''s console carrying no complaint from\nthe client. So it is not the watcher, not a stale hash, and not a failed\napply; the module simply does not re-run. Holding the edit until `onConnect`\nhad fired made no difference either, and is not kept — the bare case has no\nclient of its own, so waiting for one hangs rather than fails.\n\nThat leaves `process-update.js`, which wants the worker''s console relayed out\nto go further, and the next person should not pay for this twice: the TODO\nlists what has been eliminated. Until then `jest.retryTimes(3, {\nlogErrorsBeforeRetry: true })`, the same way the framework matrix handles its\nown upstream flake — retried, not skipped, and still logged, so it cannot\nquietly become normal.\n\nVerified on an idle machine: worker, escaping and overlay together, 72 tests,\nno retry needed. A full `test/e2e` run was attempted and abandoned — repeated\nprobe loops had left orphaned browsers behind, and unrelated suites were\ntiming out on a starved container rather than on anything in the code.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvmgACRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA0R4QAAh280pP4uKYqGWrHnMeKWzI\nZeRjWdMjB+YGRX4o4h7eqi6SizWtZWClLZoRPHXc8ZjZI3tttOg784a6lCDlB1AI\nyrFPQ99NTZHuIfqS5IyPzIQRy9eoDE7lAU90UC7tgbi67y6vDDE/BrBuWFvtIR/R\n97SAcLfS1DcHIVzit5xONqfherZqUO2V+l3du+oJPjWs3HeVcXkOidIWwC/i8EC2\nshC6lwVGoWiKpGojfQnoCr2GF231znZQ2CujgExsAQZToVCE0FPZ69yU2BIfrxQI\nYqDX1sAkjr4kIkJ8X985Cx9LLbDO8RowZQyNv2LNc8LutY3SDEzKm7OXrnVOYeWa\nDEwM+jri8c4SUBEXdZaYe1av/q+hXpnLTKQF5TJGYNd/Wq56ZCgdr1PBakvCxsaN\nrZ2BtoM7LnIRC7jOMLdYMkK3m++scL11UnWjmRMDq+5C+k1f7jJNrME2KAI0/cH8\njSdHbKegpSh4AQ8yIEknHMyp4u01kD13wVQWiDZwPnAfejnWWK4usa8y516k8qQI\ngX/sKL2S31r0j0qnzNnXL9+2lVUd13cImfytkUJQarCY25SJw2BGsFwc+10KvybB\ne1aar7PN4h3To3meqSb182RrkjZJdtiGm3+AYFA9Ws6o0HYn/QAHNrN7fZg+M6jx\nCkCZ6To8/v6PEH8sX4dw\n=AORe\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-01T14:02:41Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/da18d0790b9972972c4557eb29a83552940f0150","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKGRhMThkMDc5MGI5OTcyOTcyYzQ1NTdlYjI5YTgzNTUyOTQwZjAxNTA","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/22d2b7b81db4e9c2fbc3db238aa5c17f85df09c4","sha":"22d2b7b81db4e9c2fbc3db238aa5c17f85df09c4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/22d2b7b81db4e9c2fbc3db238aa5c17f85df09c4"}],"sha":"da18d0790b9972972c4557eb29a83552940f0150","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/da18d0790b9972972c4557eb29a83552940f0150"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/22d2b7b81db4e9c2fbc3db238aa5c17f85df09c4/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T12:06:37Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T12:06:37Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"chore(client): drop a TODO whose work shipped (#2447)\n\nThe comment asked for an `exports` field in the next major release, and\nwarned that adding one before then would be breaking because it hides every\nother path of the package.\n\nPublished 8.3.0 carries that field already, with exactly the five entries the\ncomment lists — `.`, `./client`, `./client/indicator`, `./client/overlay` and\n`./package.json` — and `main` has added `./client/sse`, `./client/ws` and\n`./client/problem` on top. So it described work that is done and warned\nagainst a break that has already happened, which is the one kind of comment\nworse than none: a reader finds it and starts planning.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"bbffbd2c51f6661a1de543994a33bc79c2262976","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/bbffbd2c51f6661a1de543994a33bc79c2262976"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/22d2b7b81db4e9c2fbc3db238aa5c17f85df09c4","verification":{"payload":"tree bbffbd2c51f6661a1de543994a33bc79c2262976\nparent 68308bee27581688357d7a08da865a244e8c0b63\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790856397 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790856397 +0300\n\nchore(client): drop a TODO whose work shipped (#2447)\n\nThe comment asked for an `exports` field in the next major release, and\nwarned that adding one before then would be breaking because it hides every\nother path of the package.\n\nPublished 8.3.0 carries that field already, with exactly the five entries the\ncomment lists — `.`, `./client`, `./client/indicator`, `./client/overlay` and\n`./package.json` — and `main` has added `./client/sse`, `./client/ws` and\n`./client/problem` on top. So it described work that is done and warned\nagainst a break that has already happened, which is the one kind of comment\nworse than none: a reader finds it and starts planning.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvkzNCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAA16YQAClv6P2NlW/fqfAdYc89i1Py\ndrclopOaxqpH7x1hXAGq35tw2sKFCUGRlygSSR3RMTui8wdJ3fbwf0J2PgZLhihP\nlD0A4y2fMsBXYCfBhFnkfkD/aRLot7OPGHFXWosivBKIUSRBjmRVSh1dAazxGE5t\nocJUgk2C5YZj1Haf93eNXScSvXK5yf3e1JjIQNWlQmvoYnCIeRqfHbNu/QuGx1Oi\nUI8jgsKIC0/FklKVQpcG2NRLZvggcOhZQ2hxaiqoHQ3MBXKuXp83x58fbIYYddra\ndqqR3ntdAnf9mAAVrUD44OiSMr5aUkKuIYhYBokmHeKxcXjJIFVT4xlkZ21OpARv\nSuDesGhoNq1sKy8GNUxvHrgKUu0R/fRa6vMPMZXQnod1PhIBo1FBjaWepq3QYXHH\nupQdthfpWSK6lYvYRk6YploUuW1iAVkW4aPSxMpjw2ABPEZ7nrBVUczpW281ZLgr\nIrAYcbtEzlR+BR2uzcX6OBIy+sUKr6ghQf8HWInlJYyV6vwh8cyKJAt2Ysjm2GyB\nx/Ia0nkWDQAN7YqL4bG1IPmij1vPEmq8XDhg3z1/6fL98xW8ztH+X3KZBN4yGQ/6\n+fpt3R1xGRwAgUStI4wkMpxTW9no/zKFnGZ5QghR1QwCCzG+edsLs1m4fS3LlEBn\n9rEvaTRJzWBCKHBvp5Mn\n=6Jqv\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-01T12:06:38Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/22d2b7b81db4e9c2fbc3db238aa5c17f85df09c4","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDIyZDJiN2I4MWRiNGU5YzJmYmMzZGIyMzhhYTVjMTdmODVkZjA5YzQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/68308bee27581688357d7a08da865a244e8c0b63","sha":"68308bee27581688357d7a08da865a244e8c0b63","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/68308bee27581688357d7a08da865a244e8c0b63"}],"sha":"22d2b7b81db4e9c2fbc3db238aa5c17f85df09c4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/22d2b7b81db4e9c2fbc3db238aa5c17f85df09c4"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/68308bee27581688357d7a08da865a244e8c0b63/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T12:05:25Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T12:05:25Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(hot): scope who may reach the hot endpoint, on both transports (new `hot.cors`) (#2444)\n\n* fix(hot): stop granting every origin access to the event stream\n\nThe Server-Sent Events endpoint answered every request with\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, inherited from `webpack-hot-middleware`.\nA payload carries a build''s module paths and, when a build fails, the source\nframes webpack puts in the error — so any site loaded in the same browser\ncould read parts of the developer''s source by opening an `EventSource` on the\nendpoint.\n\nThe grant is now asked for, through a new `hot.allowedOrigins` option: a list\nof origins, `true` for every one of them as before, or the default of none,\nwhich leaves the browser''s own same-origin rule in place. The normal setup,\nwhere the page and the middleware are the same server, needs nothing set.\n\nIt is a grant rather than a check — nothing is refused here — and it does not\napply to the `ws` transport, where a handshake is not subject to CORS.\nRefusing an upgrade stays the job of the server that owns it, through\n`onConnect(fn)` or `handleUpgrade`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* refactor(hot): name the cross-origin option cors, and default it as Vite does\n\n`hot.allowedOrigins` becomes `hot.cors`: it is a CORS grant, and the old name\nread like webpack-dev-server''s `allowedHosts`, which is a different mechanism\nthat checks the `Host` header and rejects the request.\n\nThe default is no longer \"grant nothing\" but Vite''s `server.cors` default —\n`localhost` and anything under it, `127.0.0.1` and `[::1]`, on any port and\neither scheme. A page on another port of the same machine is the one\ncross-origin case that is normal in development, and nothing a remote site can\nbe served from looks like one, so that setup keeps working while a remote\norigin still reads nothing.\n\nThe option now takes everything Vite''s does: an origin, a list of origins and\npatterns, a regular expression, a predicate, or `{ origin }`. An allowed origin\nis echoed back with `Vary: Origin` rather than wildcarded.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* feat(hot): honour cors on the WebSocket transport too\n\n`cors` only shaped the Server-Sent Events grant, which left the `ws` transport\naccepting a handshake from any origin at all — the same exposure, on the other\nwire, and the option that reads like it covers both did not.\n\nA handshake is not subject to CORS: a browser sends `Origin` and pays no\nattention to what comes back, so there the option can only be honoured by\nrefusing the upgrade. It is refused with `403` before the handshake completes,\nso nothing is ever published to a client that should not have one, and through\n`attach(server)` and `handleUpgrade(req, socket, head)` alike. The refusal is\nanswered rather than dropped, because a destroyed socket reads to a client as\nthe server going away and one that reconnects would do it forever.\n\nTwo requests are allowed whatever the option says, neither of them a page on\nanother origin reading the stream: one carrying no `Origin` at all, which is\nnot a browser but a Node client, a health check or a test; and one whose\n`Origin` is the one it was addressed to, which is the page being served.\n`EventSource` gets the second for free, since a browser knows a same-origin\nread needs no grant, but an upgrade has to work it out from the request.\n\nA server that decides for itself who may connect now sets `cors: true` so its\nown rule is the only one. The matching moves to `src/cors.js`, shared by both\ntransports, and a `transport` function of your own is handed the option too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: say what the middleware answers for, and what your server does\n\n`hot.cors` is the only protection the middleware has, and leaving the rest\nunsaid reads as though there were nothing else to do. A development server is\nreachable by anything that can reach the machine it runs on, and a bundle is\nthe developer''s source, so the line is worth drawing explicitly.\n\nWhat your server answers for, with an example of each: who may load the assets\n(`Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy`, deliberately not a default here because\nmodule federation remotes and micro-frontends load development bundles\ncross-origin on purpose, and only your server knows whether yours is one of\nthem); which hostnames you answer to, which is what makes DNS rebinding a way\nin; and `cors: true` for a server that already decides who may connect, so the\nmiddleware''s own default does not refuse first.\n\nEvery example was run as written. The last one refuses the origin its own rule\nallows if `cors: true` is dropped, which is the trap the paragraph is there to\ndescribe.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(hot): match a global or sticky cors pattern on every request\n\n`test()` on a `g` or `y` pattern leaves `lastIndex` at the end of the match,\nand `resolveCors` reads the option once and reuses what it built for every\nrequest. So a `cors` pattern carrying either flag granted and refused the same\norigin by turns:\n\n    resolveCors(/^http:\\/\\/a$/g)  asked four times  ->  true false true false\n\nWhich on the event stream means `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` appears on every\nother handshake, and on a WebSocket means every other upgrade is refused with\n`403` — a client that reconnects would succeed, fail, succeed. It reached a\nlist and `{ origin }` too, since both resolve to the same matcher. The default\npattern carries no flags and was never affected.\n\n`lastIndex` is reset before the test, and only for the two flags that read it,\nso a pattern without them is not written to at all.\n\nThree cases cover it, one per shape, each asking the same instance more than\nonce — a pattern written fresh each time would never show the bug.\n\nAlso, from the same review: the option table said `cors` covered the `sse`\nendpoint, which stopped being true when the WebSocket refusal landed, and the\n`transport` function is handed the option as it was given rather than resolved,\nunlike `path` and `heartbeat` beside it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(hot): keep the event stream''s grant as it shipped, narrow it in a major\n\nNarrowing the Server-Sent Events default to local origins is a breaking\nchange, and this release is a minor: published 8.3.0 answers every request\nwith `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` and offers no option to turn it off, so\na page served from anywhere else — a hosts-file domain, a remote dev box, a\ncontainer — would stop being able to read its own build.\n\nSo the event stream keeps granting every origin, and `hot.cors` is how to\nnarrow it today. A TODO records that the next major release makes the local\norigins its default too.\n\nThe WebSocket transport keeps the narrow default, because nothing is kept by\nloosening it: published 8.3.0''s `hot` option carries only `path`, `heartbeat`,\n`progress` and `statsOptions` — no `transport`, so the whole WebSocket wire is\nnew here and has no released behavior to preserve. It starts where the other\none is going.\n\nThe two defaults are now a table in the option''s own documentation rather\nthan one sentence, since the difference is the sort of thing that is only\nconfusing when it is implied. `CORS_LOCAL_ORIGINS` is exported so a reader can\nname today what the next major will default to, and the README says so.\n\nVerified against the published package rather than from the changelog: the\nsame build served through 8.3.0 and through this branch answers the handshake\nidentically for no `Origin`, a local one, a remote one and a LAN one — the\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` and nothing else, in all four.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"c51f12234faa2aec64cc506d278f383a689344f7","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/c51f12234faa2aec64cc506d278f383a689344f7"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/68308bee27581688357d7a08da865a244e8c0b63","verification":{"payload":"tree c51f12234faa2aec64cc506d278f383a689344f7\nparent 7462bc0425e716c5c89cef311d09854e6598da47\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790856325 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790856325 +0300\n\nfix(hot): scope who may reach the hot endpoint, on both transports (new `hot.cors`) (#2444)\n\n* fix(hot): stop granting every origin access to the event stream\n\nThe Server-Sent Events endpoint answered every request with\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, inherited from `webpack-hot-middleware`.\nA payload carries a build''s module paths and, when a build fails, the source\nframes webpack puts in the error — so any site loaded in the same browser\ncould read parts of the developer''s source by opening an `EventSource` on the\nendpoint.\n\nThe grant is now asked for, through a new `hot.allowedOrigins` option: a list\nof origins, `true` for every one of them as before, or the default of none,\nwhich leaves the browser''s own same-origin rule in place. The normal setup,\nwhere the page and the middleware are the same server, needs nothing set.\n\nIt is a grant rather than a check — nothing is refused here — and it does not\napply to the `ws` transport, where a handshake is not subject to CORS.\nRefusing an upgrade stays the job of the server that owns it, through\n`onConnect(fn)` or `handleUpgrade`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* refactor(hot): name the cross-origin option cors, and default it as Vite does\n\n`hot.allowedOrigins` becomes `hot.cors`: it is a CORS grant, and the old name\nread like webpack-dev-server''s `allowedHosts`, which is a different mechanism\nthat checks the `Host` header and rejects the request.\n\nThe default is no longer \"grant nothing\" but Vite''s `server.cors` default —\n`localhost` and anything under it, `127.0.0.1` and `[::1]`, on any port and\neither scheme. A page on another port of the same machine is the one\ncross-origin case that is normal in development, and nothing a remote site can\nbe served from looks like one, so that setup keeps working while a remote\norigin still reads nothing.\n\nThe option now takes everything Vite''s does: an origin, a list of origins and\npatterns, a regular expression, a predicate, or `{ origin }`. An allowed origin\nis echoed back with `Vary: Origin` rather than wildcarded.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* feat(hot): honour cors on the WebSocket transport too\n\n`cors` only shaped the Server-Sent Events grant, which left the `ws` transport\naccepting a handshake from any origin at all — the same exposure, on the other\nwire, and the option that reads like it covers both did not.\n\nA handshake is not subject to CORS: a browser sends `Origin` and pays no\nattention to what comes back, so there the option can only be honoured by\nrefusing the upgrade. It is refused with `403` before the handshake completes,\nso nothing is ever published to a client that should not have one, and through\n`attach(server)` and `handleUpgrade(req, socket, head)` alike. The refusal is\nanswered rather than dropped, because a destroyed socket reads to a client as\nthe server going away and one that reconnects would do it forever.\n\nTwo requests are allowed whatever the option says, neither of them a page on\nanother origin reading the stream: one carrying no `Origin` at all, which is\nnot a browser but a Node client, a health check or a test; and one whose\n`Origin` is the one it was addressed to, which is the page being served.\n`EventSource` gets the second for free, since a browser knows a same-origin\nread needs no grant, but an upgrade has to work it out from the request.\n\nA server that decides for itself who may connect now sets `cors: true` so its\nown rule is the only one. The matching moves to `src/cors.js`, shared by both\ntransports, and a `transport` function of your own is handed the option too.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* docs: say what the middleware answers for, and what your server does\n\n`hot.cors` is the only protection the middleware has, and leaving the rest\nunsaid reads as though there were nothing else to do. A development server is\nreachable by anything that can reach the machine it runs on, and a bundle is\nthe developer''s source, so the line is worth drawing explicitly.\n\nWhat your server answers for, with an example of each: who may load the assets\n(`Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy`, deliberately not a default here because\nmodule federation remotes and micro-frontends load development bundles\ncross-origin on purpose, and only your server knows whether yours is one of\nthem); which hostnames you answer to, which is what makes DNS rebinding a way\nin; and `cors: true` for a server that already decides who may connect, so the\nmiddleware''s own default does not refuse first.\n\nEvery example was run as written. The last one refuses the origin its own rule\nallows if `cors: true` is dropped, which is the trap the paragraph is there to\ndescribe.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(hot): match a global or sticky cors pattern on every request\n\n`test()` on a `g` or `y` pattern leaves `lastIndex` at the end of the match,\nand `resolveCors` reads the option once and reuses what it built for every\nrequest. So a `cors` pattern carrying either flag granted and refused the same\norigin by turns:\n\n    resolveCors(/^http:\\/\\/a$/g)  asked four times  ->  true false true false\n\nWhich on the event stream means `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` appears on every\nother handshake, and on a WebSocket means every other upgrade is refused with\n`403` — a client that reconnects would succeed, fail, succeed. It reached a\nlist and `{ origin }` too, since both resolve to the same matcher. The default\npattern carries no flags and was never affected.\n\n`lastIndex` is reset before the test, and only for the two flags that read it,\nso a pattern without them is not written to at all.\n\nThree cases cover it, one per shape, each asking the same instance more than\nonce — a pattern written fresh each time would never show the bug.\n\nAlso, from the same review: the option table said `cors` covered the `sse`\nendpoint, which stopped being true when the WebSocket refusal landed, and the\n`transport` function is handed the option as it was given rather than resolved,\nunlike `path` and `heartbeat` beside it.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(hot): keep the event stream''s grant as it shipped, narrow it in a major\n\nNarrowing the Server-Sent Events default to local origins is a breaking\nchange, and this release is a minor: published 8.3.0 answers every request\nwith `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` and offers no option to turn it off, so\na page served from anywhere else — a hosts-file domain, a remote dev box, a\ncontainer — would stop being able to read its own build.\n\nSo the event stream keeps granting every origin, and `hot.cors` is how to\nnarrow it today. A TODO records that the next major release makes the local\norigins its default too.\n\nThe WebSocket transport keeps the narrow default, because nothing is kept by\nloosening it: published 8.3.0''s `hot` option carries only `path`, `heartbeat`,\n`progress` and `statsOptions` — no `transport`, so the whole WebSocket wire is\nnew here and has no released behavior to preserve. It starts where the other\none is going.\n\nThe two defaults are now a table in the option''s own documentation rather\nthan one sentence, since the difference is the sort of thing that is only\nconfusing when it is implied. `CORS_LOCAL_ORIGINS` is exported so a reader can\nname today what the next major will default to, and the README says so.\n\nVerified against the published package rather than from the changelog: the\nsame build served through 8.3.0 and through this branch answers the handshake\nidentically for no `Origin`, a local one, a remote one and a LAN one — the\n`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` and nothing else, in all four.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvkyFCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAkHUQAG7ZZD/a3B/ZkIh+DyNk/5Bi\npw2Zzw86bTVRyDhwM/JTTNmhKP0e2WhQsMkPk8rnSYrDMyh6R5YB88yk9mtHR9pn\nylYbCxQg4GXnGk+1t8Nd1GE8ZmSMUWrZI0RD/NG/PK7ncTx5lsNRDDdrRMlIy1Mm\n6i6ZIqDBibbtwiX1isuxP69blSOUIMdyR6UNaHb/S0P9TYh374Nwj8dODlG2azkH\nDl4ferFwjuZnZpmELouhVsJytN1noEX7RUahxetxnmZqfwCG62pORFWaaHhommYx\nGnr2gIbSB0SQAuhyweWWCl9CjDJSkUvcuFHfEWRIYi5mWH3/OcoG9eyqwE899I2y\nbuuTzdFTeo5zkjbbVBHeq0G0iMuI6VFfPBGJ1LM+XDg1wT6Ueghuw7/RnxJ9B0nw\niq3FIqJGeGvyRMQ5Oe1gFaInlkStKH9K6NkTJ5hdkJjVshQze1bnpR4rlIxdQ4CT\n43A1A4+gNLfsa02xm/XANj3YZheW7uu95qgxyAC0zzYJKXM8A0Qo3Y47bcSKBSVZ\n+JGqomojfXL88xVrHR6vq8MWbFsqYoj3ok5HOsHkSXwejOkM7kM9mJ0D1r7axAUm\n8vy8EhT1RLNEbTbM33VpGbuK24NXfkGkwi+b0AEnlsem4dfuwjqjWGSMepcobzBk\n/Ui/4twuvLPz0tu6lTGf\n=wvca\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-01T12:05:25Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/68308bee27581688357d7a08da865a244e8c0b63","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDY4MzA4YmVlMjc1ODE2ODgzNTdkN2EwOGRhODY1YTI0NGU4YzBiNjM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/7462bc0425e716c5c89cef311d09854e6598da47","sha":"7462bc0425e716c5c89cef311d09854e6598da47","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/7462bc0425e716c5c89cef311d09854e6598da47"}],"sha":"68308bee27581688357d7a08da865a244e8c0b63","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/68308bee27581688357d7a08da865a244e8c0b63"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/7462bc0425e716c5c89cef311d09854e6598da47/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-10-01T09:24:46Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-10-01T09:24:46Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(middleware): guard the stripped remainder against traversal (#2445)\n\n`getFilenameFromUrl` tested the whole request path for `..` and then, after\njoining, checked that the result had landed inside the output root. The first\nof those catches far less than it looks like it does, because it tests the\n*normalized* path: `/public/../secret` has become `secret` by then, with no\n`..` segment left to match, and `/assets../secret` never had a `..` segment at\nall — it is a sibling that merely shares the prefix, which a `publicPath`\nwithout a trailing slash makes reachable.\n\nSo both classes of traversal were held off by the containment check alone, one\nblock standing between a crafted url and the filesystem. Removing it by hand\nleaks a file above the output root through five different spellings, under both\na slash-terminated `publicPath` and one without.\n\nThe remainder left after the prefix is stripped is now checked for `..` on its\nown, before it is joined onto anything. Two independent guards: one on the\nshape of the request, one on where it resolved. The containment check stays as\nthe backstop for an encoding or platform quirk that gets past the shapes, and\nthe comments now say which is which.\n\nA `..` that walks out of the output root and straight back into it\n(`/assets../dist/file.js`, resolving to a file that is inside) is refused\nrather than served, which is the one behavior change. It was only ever a second\nspelling of a path reachable directly, and a spelling indistinguishable in\nshape from an escape.\n\nReplayed both published advisories afterwards — GHSA-g84c-rxfj-3j2c''s\n`/assets../secret` and GHSA-wr3j-pwj9-hqq6''s\n`/public/..%2f..%2f..%2f..%2f../etc/passwd` — along with fourteen other\nspellings: every one refused or missed, and legitimate paths still served.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"942bd77519c44ab933943f3e5487d09f2e1437d4","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/942bd77519c44ab933943f3e5487d09f2e1437d4"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/7462bc0425e716c5c89cef311d09854e6598da47","verification":{"payload":"tree 942bd77519c44ab933943f3e5487d09f2e1437d4\nparent 94935acd751cf5283b7ce544568e8e02a20ef2dd\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790846686 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790846686 +0300\n\nfix(middleware): guard the stripped remainder against traversal (#2445)\n\n`getFilenameFromUrl` tested the whole request path for `..` and then, after\njoining, checked that the result had landed inside the output root. The first\nof those catches far less than it looks like it does, because it tests the\n*normalized* path: `/public/../secret` has become `secret` by then, with no\n`..` segment left to match, and `/assets../secret` never had a `..` segment at\nall — it is a sibling that merely shares the prefix, which a `publicPath`\nwithout a trailing slash makes reachable.\n\nSo both classes of traversal were held off by the containment check alone, one\nblock standing between a crafted url and the filesystem. Removing it by hand\nleaks a file above the output root through five different spellings, under both\na slash-terminated `publicPath` and one without.\n\nThe remainder left after the prefix is stripped is now checked for `..` on its\nown, before it is joined onto anything. Two independent guards: one on the\nshape of the request, one on where it resolved. The containment check stays as\nthe backstop for an encoding or platform quirk that gets past the shapes, and\nthe comments now say which is which.\n\nA `..` that walks out of the output root and straight back into it\n(`/assets../dist/file.js`, resolving to a file that is inside) is refused\nrather than served, which is the one behavior change. It was only ever a second\nspelling of a path reachable directly, and a spelling indistinguishable in\nshape from an escape.\n\nReplayed both published advisories afterwards — GHSA-g84c-rxfj-3j2c''s\n`/assets../secret` and GHSA-wr3j-pwj9-hqq6''s\n`/public/..%2f..%2f..%2f..%2f../etc/passwd` — along with fourteen other\nspellings: every one refused or missed, and legitimate paths still served.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvibeCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAfyYQAKEMLB0Ql+41twKGKRu/IbT6\nq1w88DdT6aMvG6QggQbRsP5C3WRr8rpt8JW7YnFgFh5JDUc9lBVkIUpSHEfdam6m\nOuEuVP2ypiiAuSiSYzc9xQNorAR2+j2Mgxq3kTvI26KB0eaA4MpctqMM82w6x0Te\nHedtQsbuzJl6w23mVY37+v370xi2+WB0DDiySTWQVJQqdtdQj0BHssGK1WwhIbYq\nEnW9zxAg5klaymj0BfuqQibOJXJcltkBXVNci98mABBd+Hc+9MJ/lVitvHsWSK0I\nRGBCbEL1k3R+EULB56UAtFGitWt63EDi7rZi8iNzKpimGc4V57WAl/yl41HwNoiO\nfAk4+OfPgtxfbYLlm58B1+LdgdlkCvIPgvo7WHhGruZGAl5Yquckf38FO7tZivv/\nTAs+a8Tvc/zwstBBY4f0hsHl0dbQ9QIezYU3nVmoMCIKI0Cl9IPtvcR4e09hq4md\ny2Peqk40kL+qGZPvPVXdDx0zrxQSGURXTrMMHXGVCDj67VMRRAlw31XAFva/yER0\nvwukKQMrKO0Hnh9ppFE4fSFWR0is4ENR1CWx7F0kTqZZCDb3OkDHvVmGfqzw0+U3\nX09gpjCZe24TYbK7OPzO4roTosuMCVyYJRSp5lofIKpCJ6vbAH8XKLgOnCzCpzVJ\nnNBIGN+0eyYzGnIN1RZA\n=00Fy\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-10-01T09:24:47Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/7462bc0425e716c5c89cef311d09854e6598da47","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDc0NjJiYzA0MjVlNzE2YzVjODljZWYzMTFkMDk4NTRlNjU5OGRhNDc","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/94935acd751cf5283b7ce544568e8e02a20ef2dd","sha":"94935acd751cf5283b7ce544568e8e02a20ef2dd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/94935acd751cf5283b7ce544568e8e02a20ef2dd"}],"sha":"7462bc0425e716c5c89cef311d09854e6598da47","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/7462bc0425e716c5c89cef311d09854e6598da47"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/94935acd751cf5283b7ce544568e8e02a20ef2dd/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-30T16:15:29Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-30T16:15:29Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"chore: say what dropping the validation fallback is worth (#2443)\n\nThe TODO said to bump the peer range and remove the fallback, but not what\nthat buys: `schema-utils` is only reachable through it. From webpack 5.106 on,\n`compiler.validate` is the whole of what validation needs, and webpack carries\nits own copy of `schema-utils` to do it with — so raising the peer range takes\na production dependency out of this package rather than only tidying a branch.\n\nNamed the version, and the `Schema` typedef that goes with it.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"cfebb57a52f8059b91ac8d119e9d95ad6428e378","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/cfebb57a52f8059b91ac8d119e9d95ad6428e378"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/94935acd751cf5283b7ce544568e8e02a20ef2dd","verification":{"payload":"tree cfebb57a52f8059b91ac8d119e9d95ad6428e378\nparent 187f623b070ef654911b36fd298da4b9f0c87e84\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790784929 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790784929 +0300\n\nchore: say what dropping the validation fallback is worth (#2443)\n\nThe TODO said to bump the peer range and remove the fallback, but not what\nthat buys: `schema-utils` is only reachable through it. From webpack 5.106 on,\n`compiler.validate` is the whole of what validation needs, and webpack carries\nits own copy of `schema-utils` to do it with — so raising the peer range takes\na production dependency out of this package rather than only tidying a branch.\n\nNamed the version, and the `Schema` typedef that goes with it.\n\n\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvTWhCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAC+UQACNY+p4KJGi6oxG1BcfvyQhu\nyvbY5vsOdppXSSlQ+BIlwGS9Ug/ugJmLYegq60BIQKVK4eKdTQCp5LuTe1Rdka59\n7Is/ZFL8JHw3e9qsAJDlsNJQawi65QaCD9JOSMprPqn+d1F2uHBxnh2znzuZcYmr\nEdHUCbtYf9Uu58RmKiZe6H0bYZK6+lCHYb6HzIhxfV0DCxVGU7uNwZsQQ24aopbP\nqbjgOje3w4FsMG5oAcQ+g2SXTNDvpsRAFiHRJyvy9fg3f9zYfq7xU9BfMEHxUpTL\nn6dTVkut/IrV8P2FuhT9iuC6aS4XYdj5oFaZnfcIx8sV/HvweB+sqesRIQrPLniq\nWoJemURkX2ht5NCo2qGSadwwGo1gtYqIpI25oIH0nXGjgg1SVAuwq6Pa964BmNsu\nBUSpZBL3qEOQ23FByjzHl+fLdv+B4Yd0S9FvJDt8e0Y/Mi/M4zNcfhGM0svvKcA7\nFnCLZDfvTKklKg5bbvDXPvt4pja3Dg6Z3H7LkCCA9S9FGJdcGj+MuOpjjMkvF3xU\n/4G7fuOS4w26oKps/oYLpGIWeaISM78aA05jqvcotcBgr0amn0i61jJkg1r9qP+S\nZ/cECrhiJgX2FHwE/AH+mtAlXUCOMI/Vu9p9N3RqamFPTSBKJNh1wdmEa+GX7cM7\nSaOgpaYl2nr65a/cj9KJ\n=kyR4\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-30T16:15:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/94935acd751cf5283b7ce544568e8e02a20ef2dd","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDk0OTM1YWNkNzUxY2Y1MjgzYjdjZTU0NDU2OGU4ZTAyYTIwZWYyZGQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/187f623b070ef654911b36fd298da4b9f0c87e84","sha":"187f623b070ef654911b36fd298da4b9f0c87e84","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/187f623b070ef654911b36fd298da4b9f0c87e84"}],"sha":"94935acd751cf5283b7ce544568e8e02a20ef2dd","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/94935acd751cf5283b7ce544568e8e02a20ef2dd"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/187f623b070ef654911b36fd298da4b9f0c87e84/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-30T16:14:59Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-30T16:14:59Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix(client): recognize a file reference wherever a stack puts one (#2439)\n\n* fix(client): recognize a file reference wherever a stack puts one\n\nThe overlay matched `(\\.{1,2}\\/[\\w./-]+\\.\\w+)` — webpack''s own relative paths\nand nothing else. A runtime error''s stack carries more than that, so its frames\noffered nothing to open. Absolute paths, Windows paths with a drive letter, and\nany of those behind a `file://` url are recognized now.\n\nTwo things fall out of matching more:\n\n  * A url''s path is not somewhere an editor can go. The `/app.js` of\n    `https://example.test/app.js` would have become an offer to open, so a\n    reference is matched with the character before it and a url''s path is left\n    to `linkify`. Matched rather than looked behind, which the ES5 baseline\n    this file compiles to has no syntax for.\n  * A frame in webpack''s generated runtime has no file behind it, and reaches\n    the overlay as an absolute path. That line is left alone, the way Rsbuild\n    leaves it alone. Node''s internals need no entry: a real frame for one\n    carries no file extension, so a reference is never recognized in it.\n\nAn editor that does not open now says so. The click fired a `fetch` and\nignored how it went, so a reader who clicked a reference and saw nothing\nhappen had no way to tell whether they had missed it. The reference gets a\ntitle saying it could not be opened, and the console gets the reason and which\nendpoint was asked.\n\nAlso added the test the strict-`style-src` path never had. `normalizeInlineStyles`\nexists because a CSP without `''unsafe-inline''` makes the parser drop the\n`style` attributes `ansi-html` and the highlighters produce, and it re-applies\nthem through the CSSOM — but nothing proved it, so it could have rotted\nsilently.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(client): keep a stack frame on the line it came on\n\nThe replacement for a located reference ended in a newline. That was invisible\nwhile only webpack''s own end-of-line header matched it, and broke every stack\nframe once more than that did: the match stops at the position, so the closing\nbracket of `at one (./src/app.js:3:1)` — and anything after it — was pushed\nonto a line of its own.\n\nFound by CodeRabbit on #2439.\n\nNothing depended on the newline: the whole suite passed with it gone, which is\nalso why nothing would have caught it coming back. A test asserts a frame''s\ntext now, and fails with the newline restored on its own.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"60b3ee7ffb8e0733ffa1079b585e610dbebf0e27","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/60b3ee7ffb8e0733ffa1079b585e610dbebf0e27"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/187f623b070ef654911b36fd298da4b9f0c87e84","verification":{"payload":"tree 60b3ee7ffb8e0733ffa1079b585e610dbebf0e27\nparent 018e2193307b95aa592dbd44ba32cdc5fe76a7b1\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790784899 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790784899 +0300\n\nfix(client): recognize a file reference wherever a stack puts one (#2439)\n\n* fix(client): recognize a file reference wherever a stack puts one\n\nThe overlay matched `(\\.{1,2}\\/[\\w./-]+\\.\\w+)` — webpack''s own relative paths\nand nothing else. A runtime error''s stack carries more than that, so its frames\noffered nothing to open. Absolute paths, Windows paths with a drive letter, and\nany of those behind a `file://` url are recognized now.\n\nTwo things fall out of matching more:\n\n  * A url''s path is not somewhere an editor can go. The `/app.js` of\n    `https://example.test/app.js` would have become an offer to open, so a\n    reference is matched with the character before it and a url''s path is left\n    to `linkify`. Matched rather than looked behind, which the ES5 baseline\n    this file compiles to has no syntax for.\n  * A frame in webpack''s generated runtime has no file behind it, and reaches\n    the overlay as an absolute path. That line is left alone, the way Rsbuild\n    leaves it alone. Node''s internals need no entry: a real frame for one\n    carries no file extension, so a reference is never recognized in it.\n\nAn editor that does not open now says so. The click fired a `fetch` and\nignored how it went, so a reader who clicked a reference and saw nothing\nhappen had no way to tell whether they had missed it. The reference gets a\ntitle saying it could not be opened, and the console gets the reason and which\nendpoint was asked.\n\nAlso added the test the strict-`style-src` path never had. `normalizeInlineStyles`\nexists because a CSP without `''unsafe-inline''` makes the parser drop the\n`style` attributes `ansi-html` and the highlighters produce, and it re-applies\nthem through the CSSOM — but nothing proved it, so it could have rotted\nsilently.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(client): keep a stack frame on the line it came on\n\nThe replacement for a located reference ended in a newline. That was invisible\nwhile only webpack''s own end-of-line header matched it, and broke every stack\nframe once more than that did: the match stops at the position, so the closing\nbracket of `at one (./src/app.js:3:1)` — and anything after it — was pushed\nonto a line of its own.\n\nFound by CodeRabbit on #2439.\n\nNothing depended on the newline: the whole suite passed with it gone, which is\nalso why nothing would have caught it coming back. A test asserts a frame''s\ntext now, and fails with the newline restored on its own.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvTWDCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAewMQAHXy5CDaCNkIFlprD0zU7YVT\ndchXH73J0QnHO6K1gdunIuC8Z59gjIPH95i2O7/+ztaN4OxgTQ+u8M8ngBSIEG+3\njyW2jXdNLocpGbbzeDFuRll7XWz4ivUcRQXaXMx3yPIT139myrqJMDPNUDIennbQ\nCyyU/BBP1QGR2rTOIJYP2P5/jOIqUahOQ2nuZpmwIuOikN7G8SY9Ab2JFSCpuQEV\nz+oEs6DNg//SPEZfxYPGBgVqgNcXusX36oA+/l7efFXdmszbeMFbVww5wnfxUDJm\nkBpRSIqphr5YjLQK2/RTMO3mkGTvH8LhU2XCKeaA6A4Qqup3SDsNg6+/Qcdur8iq\no30SDGRhleAyRBYdSwQOMN6VR0CL7LdVNUHw/s3QYToxnlm838WZQ/Mv1y2/7tFP\n5HJodQ4ScTUbZigM9FaBWEzt7OWVrei6k4CrAldeW01DCt1mSSbJ3gnjm7xCkBLX\nIIB7Pkxh3RMLHq087yiMz+woTAccMW9N5jakQGymXDIj1IgB323dYmDSU8tlWYmT\nRq6BO+5sYMPHtWLFor4PLF0DqTHfx2YOU0SGUHjlmkuQn10tB0wtPu1mvdIlonlX\nXdQa5Y2pFyhFDRWlF1R3B7ekr61Os+iZQrSdtV8ZRebNEXOlWRIzn11keuwsfoid\n6OsILdABUT235Cnp4riB\n=fDrm\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-30T16:14:59Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/187f623b070ef654911b36fd298da4b9f0c87e84","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDE4N2Y2MjNiMDcwZWY2NTQ5MTFiMzZmZDI5OGRhNGI5ZjBjODdlODQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/018e2193307b95aa592dbd44ba32cdc5fe76a7b1","sha":"018e2193307b95aa592dbd44ba32cdc5fe76a7b1","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/018e2193307b95aa592dbd44ba32cdc5fe76a7b1"}],"sha":"187f623b070ef654911b36fd298da4b9f0c87e84","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/187f623b070ef654911b36fd298da4b9f0c87e84"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/4567934?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/alexander-akait","id":4567934,"login":"alexander-akait","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ1Njc5MzQ=","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/alexander-akait","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commits/018e2193307b95aa592dbd44ba32cdc5fe76a7b1/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-30T12:34:43Z","email":"4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Akait"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-30T12:34:43Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"refactor(client): inline the ANSI conversion, drop a dependency (#2442)\n\n* refactor(client): inline the ANSI conversion, drop a dependency\n\n`ansi-html-community` was a third used and two thirds unused: the `tags`\ngetters and their pre-ES5 fallback, a `reset()` nobody called, and the\nvalidation branches a fixed palette cannot reach. All of it shipped into every\nconsumer''s browser bundle, from a package whose last release was 0.0.8 in\nApril 2022 — itself a fork of the abandoned `ansi-html`. Four production\ndependencies now instead of five.\n\nChecked against the package over a corpus before replacing it: identical for\nevery sequence a build actually produces. I confirmed what that is rather than\nassuming — a real babel-loader failure with colour forced emits\n`[0m [1m [22m [31m [33m [36m [39m [90m]`, all single-parameter.\n\nFour things it got wrong, each now tested:\n\n  * `\"transparent\"`, which is how the palette says to leave the page''s own\n    colour alone, became `color:#transparent`. Not a colour — so the reset\n    worked only because a browser drops an invalid declaration, and the\n    inverse sequence did nothing whatsoever.\n  * A sequence with more than one parameter (`\\u001b[1;31m`) matched nothing,\n    leaving the escape in the output as text in front of the reader.\n  * `\\u001b[m`, which is `\\u001b[0m` written short, was left the same way.\n  * A closing sequence with nothing open emitted an unmatched `</span>`. The\n    highlighters wrap their own spans around this output, so a stray close\n    could end one of theirs early.\n\nOne trap worth recording: the package initialised its tag tables at module\nload via `reset()`, and `setColors` here is only reached when `ansiColors` is\nconfigured. Without an unconditional call at load, ANSI would have silently\nstopped converting for everyone who never set a palette — which the corpus\ncomparison could not have caught, since it configures the palette itself.\n\nThe conversion had no test while it was a dependency. It has 19.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(client): close what is actually open, not always a span\n\nThe stack held SGR parameters and every close emitted `</span>`. So an\nelement opened as a tag was closed as a span — `\\u001b[3mx` gave `<i>x</span>`\n— and an interleaved sequence crossed its tags: `\\u001b[3m\\u001b[31mx\\u001b[23m`\ngave `<i><span style=\"…\">x</i></span>`.\n\nBoth are inherited from the package this replaced, identically, but leaving\nthem sat badly next to a changeset claiming to fix unmatched closes. This is\nthe same class of defect.\n\nEach open element now carries its own closing tag. A close emits the innermost\none, a repeated parameter closes back through everything opened inside it, and\nthe final cleanup unwinds in reverse. Which parameter a closer belongs to is\nstill not tracked — `[3m[31mx[23m` cannot close the italic without crossing\nthe colour span, so the italic runs to the end of the message — but nothing\nthis produces fails to nest, which is what matters when the highlighters wrap\ntheir own spans around it afterwards.\n\nFound by CodeRabbit on #2442.\n\nFive cases for the reported shapes, and one that walks every three-sequence\ncombination of twelve sequences and checks each closing tag matches what is\ninnermost. Six of the 25 fail without this.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","tree":{"sha":"32147e9562563cd336cdbc9ffd58b8c1451f839e","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/trees/32147e9562563cd336cdbc9ffd58b8c1451f839e"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/git/commits/018e2193307b95aa592dbd44ba32cdc5fe76a7b1","verification":{"payload":"tree 32147e9562563cd336cdbc9ffd58b8c1451f839e\nparent 8a6556e8c47669a534efdfd04e3243bdb13752fb\nauthor Alexander Akait <4567934+alexander-akait@users.noreply.github.com> 1790771683 +0300\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790771683 +0300\n\nrefactor(client): inline the ANSI conversion, drop a dependency (#2442)\n\n* refactor(client): inline the ANSI conversion, drop a dependency\n\n`ansi-html-community` was a third used and two thirds unused: the `tags`\ngetters and their pre-ES5 fallback, a `reset()` nobody called, and the\nvalidation branches a fixed palette cannot reach. All of it shipped into every\nconsumer''s browser bundle, from a package whose last release was 0.0.8 in\nApril 2022 — itself a fork of the abandoned `ansi-html`. Four production\ndependencies now instead of five.\n\nChecked against the package over a corpus before replacing it: identical for\nevery sequence a build actually produces. I confirmed what that is rather than\nassuming — a real babel-loader failure with colour forced emits\n`[0m [1m [22m [31m [33m [36m [39m [90m]`, all single-parameter.\n\nFour things it got wrong, each now tested:\n\n  * `\"transparent\"`, which is how the palette says to leave the page''s own\n    colour alone, became `color:#transparent`. Not a colour — so the reset\n    worked only because a browser drops an invalid declaration, and the\n    inverse sequence did nothing whatsoever.\n  * A sequence with more than one parameter (`\\u001b[1;31m`) matched nothing,\n    leaving the escape in the output as text in front of the reader.\n  * `\\u001b[m`, which is `\\u001b[0m` written short, was left the same way.\n  * A closing sequence with nothing open emitted an unmatched `</span>`. The\n    highlighters wrap their own spans around this output, so a stray close\n    could end one of theirs early.\n\nOne trap worth recording: the package initialised its tag tables at module\nload via `reset()`, and `setColors` here is only reached when `ansiColors` is\nconfigured. Without an unconditional call at load, ANSI would have silently\nstopped converting for everyone who never set a palette — which the corpus\ncomparison could not have caught, since it configures the palette itself.\n\nThe conversion had no test while it was a dependency. It has 19.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n* fix(client): close what is actually open, not always a span\n\nThe stack held SGR parameters and every close emitted `</span>`. So an\nelement opened as a tag was closed as a span — `\\u001b[3mx` gave `<i>x</span>`\n— and an interleaved sequence crossed its tags: `\\u001b[3m\\u001b[31mx\\u001b[23m`\ngave `<i><span style=\"…\">x</i></span>`.\n\nBoth are inherited from the package this replaced, identically, but leaving\nthem sat badly next to a changeset claiming to fix unmatched closes. This is\nthe same class of defect.\n\nEach open element now carries its own closing tag. A close emits the innermost\none, a repeated parameter closes back through everything opened inside it, and\nthe final cleanup unwinds in reverse. Which parameter a closer belongs to is\nstill not tracked — `[3m[31mx[23m` cannot close the italic without crossing\nthe colour span, so the italic runs to the end of the message — but nothing\nthis produces fails to nest, which is what matters when the highlighters wrap\ntheir own spans around it afterwards.\n\nFound by CodeRabbit on #2442.\n\nFive cases for the reported shapes, and one that walks every three-sequence\ncombination of twelve sequences and checks each closing tag matches what is\ninnermost. Six of the 25 fail without this.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01UjuMAuk9o6UazjHzcAQCTA\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqvQHjCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAuowQAAE38eyl11YYug+05SG2EpLm\nsxnTjc6JwrZumDcceUKKu8+1aHCx0k6KBX/JjNRgOmjZM9UWvJftp8LSSO/YiCTU\n8TQBnjFWDIAsVRj3JA/MAnTWSyx0hl3OvV/oBwaVDrLk7Ek+u2V4bUvRvPbKURCs\naHBlXRA+jFHqZ8gBba4oqva96ahxUr6+OlEzLOoBa6ICEGc4+RKmbenY6A6F/EQA\nouuQn8ZedOhFlOiyIFT9zscLFwTEsM4y+8hnfgFR+aARDM6AD1bOun5eBtkJEmet\n5b+0T7O4hQexK2hipJLkb8Tu5YSd33i6E1zF7wzXfDDSxKSqIh8Qc5cuWlNI0WAp\nhpgvMcKGYXA47AiB68UlAPQfQC2/b/ax+Kyd7iag+v4mMypsCJ4r+eF9Bf2SMfGH\nZSU2gijFEsvO8ZBnGK8lHZBUPAHBP9ycWOaziKHqFIaR90MudMkSJLPWgwHLwSH4\nEEK6rOlZf3UhIBRlLH76axPJHpj3fJSWhfnCX/Cm6KRjWzoDRYmOtSwZyGFku/P6\n9gmfuwzZtsAWJXGVPg9sqBSrQhJ3QBC0J2vEYR1F5BrAAN42DBWHhS5XH+PS1O+d\ncScBxwqUaaZ3D2hzYNY3jR7n49vCsYHEi3URkSyxUQ+uUUSgZfRnh1clmNjkmqG8\nQTHdwcx+WGRSrsD7RcCK\n=EfR+\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-30T12:34:44Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/018e2193307b95aa592dbd44ba32cdc5fe76a7b1","node_id":"C_kwDOAFGXAtoAKDAxOGUyMTkzMzA3Yjk1YWE1OTJkYmQ0NGJhMzJjZGM1ZmU3NmE3YjE","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/webpack/webpack-dev-middleware/commit/8a6556e8c47669a534efdfd04e3243bdb13752fb","sha":"8a6556e8c47669a