count row were\nonly checked for existence, letting a reseller grant DomainAdmin rights\non another tenant''s domain. Check access like the JSON API does.\n\nReported-by: Wenhao Wu (Southeast University)","tree":{"sha":"fdc40de8bbe3641d660e644d80865f38097f9649","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modoboa/modoboa/git/trees/fdc40de8bbe3641d660e644d80865f38097f9649"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modoboa/modoboa/git/commits/3e7455c4f461ea89c2395316663074fbbc452553","verification":{"payload":"tree fdc40de8bbe3641d660e644d80865f38097f9649\nparent d3826a587fa604e710b779c14199c04a6e43e487\nauthor Antoine Nguyen <tonio@ngyn.org> 1790056530 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790056530 +0200\n\nfix(admin): check domain access on CSV import of domain admins (#4214)\n\nThe extra administered domains columns of an imported account row were\nonly checked for existence, letting a reseller grant DomainAdmin rights\non another tenant''s domain. Check access like the JSON API does.\n\nReported-by: Wenhao Wu (Southeast University)","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqshhSCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAFwQQAEMWju0SCGwK3CZkmfViesSM\no7m4RlvByL2ec5poTwUEC8dpKA4tuOp8bHPsNaY2MYBxhSx0stF78Muj3LoTcQI4\nuYG8PRmXojx3KXV3mIkN8xyXzdorJQxDfX6vm/otcn7jrHHO38G+kjeWeKtcqa/i\neKrs5wmFoPO3z2P3oI8madDtdwJ9qLJ/qDraYWYr6OGbv2mO7wfnGYWmA/h/vrNQ\nrIfazpLhJGGJab/JIqAKJtD0uG+KgIHmPXbqXSTSy9LLdltPHngfdkAHhiz9Fu99\nl9u1TVny9BoX0WAG5Y2fb18cVIXYfDaB/tHJJB8yDgIOtCccyBGDkikuX8atWrIo\nBUTFpwKXXmCNWapc57gSsBgvwT2aA6IlQKOxLKedRehRlKH3WcqZoVHh2i85s/7R\nXrmBrXVe9leogjvd+MErImWeru+YjrPGXtmJ+j6Tzqt+xTIUz+SAa7rssw5EGbR2\nuSY+PfbIBSQtFSeF47/k2eldouvD6qdLmA6kTlY8D6sRYIVaSn4wJ2QhktYceGUr\nav6oI8UxXixcieUu1D4tkyDD8rAxIklPMLBpGuB9folbLtPUr1IMOnJm3ZkxZVK1\nN3ZQHrGibFq2H0WDaHTVUG0LCwquUuSS6umfXBcIn8K6ZA76ybAQ9YIka9/eHpg2\nHTNoVoDTC/vtQG2fQ6re\n=Rq9Z\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-22T05:55:30Z"}},"committer":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/19864447?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/web-flow","id":19864447,"login":"web-flow","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE5ODY0NDQ3","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/modoboa/modoboa/commit/3e7455c4f461ea89c2395316663074fbbc452553","node_id":"C_kwDOAJB9V9oAKDNlNzQ1NWM0ZjQ2MWVhODljMjM5NTMxNjY2MzA3NGZiYmM0NTI1NTM","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/modoboa/modoboa/commit/d3826a587fa604e710b779c14199c04a6e43e487","sha":"d3826a587fa604e710b779c14199c04a6e43e487","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modoboa/modoboa/commits/d3826a587fa604e710b779c14199c04a6e43e487"}],"sha":"3e7455c4f461ea89c2395316663074fbbc452553","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modoboa/modoboa/commits/3e7455c4f461ea89c2395316663074fbbc452553"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/531694?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tonioo/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tonioo/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tonioo/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tonioo/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/tonioo","id":531694,"login":"tonioo","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjUzMTY5NA==","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tonioo/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tonioo/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tonioo/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tonioo/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/tonioo/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/tonioo","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modoboa/modoboa/commits/d3826a587fa604e710b779c14199c04a6e43e487/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-21T18:16:41Z","email":"tonio@ngyn.org","name":"Antoine Nguyen"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-21T18:16:41Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"feat(webmail): conversation view and message display redesign (#4212)\n\n* feat(webmail): list messages as threads\n\nAdd support for the IMAP THREAD extension (RFC 5256) so that messages\ncan be grouped into conversations:\n\n* `parse_thread_response()` flattens the nested reply, ignoring an\n  unexpected one instead of raising;\n* `threads_count()` ranks the threads with the order already computed\n  by `messages_count()`, which works with and without SORT;\n* `fetch_threads()` summarizes a page of threads, going through the\n  same FETCH code path as the flat listing;\n* two read-only endpoints, `threads` and `thread`, which report\n  `threading_supported` when the server lacks the extension.\n\nThe flat listing is left untouched.\n\n* feat(webmail): add a listing mode preference\n\nA new \"listing_mode\" user preference chooses between the flat listing\nand conversations. It defaults to \"flat\", so nothing changes for\nexisting users.\n\n* feat(webmail): switch the message list to conversations\n\nThe messages list can now group messages into conversations:\n\n* the message card moves to its own EmailListItem component, so that\n  the list doesn''t mix both modes;\n* ThreadListItem displays a conversation and expands it on demand;\n* a toggle in the toolbar switches modes and saves the preference. It\n  is disabled where conversations make no sense (drafts, scheduled\n  messages) or when the server lacks the THREAD extension.\n\nSelecting a conversation selects all its messages, so the existing bulk\nactions apply to the whole thread without any API change.\n\n* feat(webmail): add a conversation view\n\nClicking a conversation now opens every message of the thread, each in\nits own collapsible panel. Unread messages and the last one are\nexpanded right away; the others load their body when opened, which\nmarks them as read, as before.\n\nThe message body moves to an EmailMessageBody component: the single\niframe of EmailView was absolutely positioned and sized by hand against\nthe window, which can''t work with several messages. Each frame now sits\nin the normal flow and takes the height of its content.\n\nMeasuring that content needs the frame to be same origin, so\n''allow-same-origin'' joins the sandbox. Scripts stay forbidden by the\nsandbox and by the CSP, and a document that cannot execute anything\ncannot use that origin either.\n\n* fix(webmail): don''t group unrelated messages into a conversation\n\nTHREAD=REFERENCES follows the reply chain, then applies the last step\nof RFC 5256: root messages sharing the same base subject are merged\ninto a single thread. Two unrelated messages called \"Invoice\" ended up\nin the same conversation, which is not what mail clients display.\n\nAsk for THREAD=REFS instead, the same algorithm without that step, and\nkeep REFERENCES as a fallback for servers that lack it.\n\n* feat(webmail): make the messages list readable at a glance\n\nThe listing displayed one card per message, with full size controls and\nthe sender stacked above the subject: around 90px per message, nothing\naligned from one row to the next, and unread messages signalled only by\na bold font.\n\nIt is now a single flat list of 40px rows with fixed columns, so the\neye scans a column instead of redrawing a boundary at every message:\n\n* a coloured bar on the left edge marks what hasn''t been read;\n* sender, subject and date sit in their own column, dates in tabular\n  figures;\n* the message size leaves the row: the paperclip already says there is\n  an attachment, and the size is on the message itself;\n* dates keep only what tells messages apart, the time today, the\n  weekday this week, then the day and month, and the year only when it\n  isn''t the current one;\n* a conversation shows \"▸3\" and its first two participants instead of a\n  chip, a full list of names and an \"unread\" counter.\n\n* fix(webmail): widen the date column and show the conversation control\n\nThe date column was 76px wide and allowed to wrap, so the longest\nformats went to a second line. Measured in a browser, the widest real\ndate is 85px (\"Sat 12:00 p.m.\", English with a weekday and a 12 hour\nclock) and 84px in Russian; the column is now 104px and never wraps.\n\nThe number of messages of a conversation was plain text, with nothing\nsaying it could be clicked. It becomes a small button, and it now sits\nin a column of its own, always present: a thread holding a single\nmessage used to have no such cell at all, which shifted its subject\naway from the others.\n\n* fix(webmail): don''t render messages as conversations while loading\n\nReloading a page in conversation mode raised \"props.thread.uids is\nundefined\": the listing was fetched flat before the preference was\nread, then the preference switched the display to conversations while\nthose flat results were still on screen, and a message has no uids.\n\nWhat is displayed now follows the results that came back, not the\npreference. The first listing also waits for the preference instead of\nbeing fetched flat and immediately fetched again, which removes both\nthe flash and one request per page load.\n\n* feat(webmail): align the listing toolbar with the list\n\nThe toolbar was an elevated card holding a 64px v-toolbar and filled\ncolour blocks, above a list that had become a flat sheet of 40px rows:\ntwo different surfaces, and three saturated buttons shouting over a\nlist whose only colour is the unread bar.\n\nIt is now the same surface as the list, with the same radius and the\nsame hairline border and no elevation. Actions keep their meaning\nthrough the colour of their icon rather than a filled block, the\nsearch field drops its outline for a filled field, and the page range\nis set in tabular figures.\n\nIts checkbox is padded by the width of the accent bar and one gap, so\nit sits exactly above the checkboxes of the rows.\n\n* feat(webmail): show the bulk actions only when messages are selected\n\nDelete, junk and the flag menu were always on display although they do\nnothing until something is selected. They now take the place of the\nsearch field while a selection is on, next to the number of selected\nmessages, and the search field comes back when it is cleared.\n\nThe mode toggle and the page controls keep their place in all cases,\nso nothing moves under the pointer. Emptying a mailbox leaves that\nmenu: it acts on the folder, not on a selection, so it becomes a\nbutton of its own, only in the trash.\n\nThe checkbox also reports a partial selection, and the selection is\ndropped when changing page or mailbox, where it would have applied to\nmessages that are no longer displayed.\n\n* feat(webmail): widen the listing search field\n\nThe field kept the 280px it had when the bulk actions sat next to it.\nIt now takes the room they left, up to 480px, past which a search\nfield stops being readable, and never goes below 180px.\n\n* feat(webmail): describe attachments from the body structure\n\nAttachments were only known by name, decoded with a loop that matched\nany parameter starting with \"name\" and ignored RFC 2231: accented or\nlong file names, split into encoded sections, came out garbled.\n\nThe MIME parameters are now decoded properly (RFC 2047 words, RFC 2231\ncharsets and numbered sections), and each attachment is described by\nits name, size and content type, all read from the body structure. The\nlisting already fetches it, so every message now carries its\nattachment list without its body being loaded.\n\n* fix(webmail): stop listing embedded images as attachments\n\nWhen a message was a multipart/related at its top level, the most\ncommon shape of an HTML message with a logo or a signature, the\nsubtype was lost on the way down to its parts: its images were listed\nas attachments and their cid: references were never resolved.\n\nParts are also sorted on more than their type. A text part is an\nattachment when its disposition says so, and an image is embedded when\nit can be displayed and carries a Content-ID, whether it sits in a\nmultipart/related or is marked inline in a multipart/mixed, as some\nclients do. Anything else, a PDF inside a multipart/related included,\nis an attachment.\n\nOnce the body is loaded, the embedded images it doesn''t reference, or\nall of them when the plain text is displayed, are listed with the\nattachments: they could not be seen otherwise. They can be downloaded\nlike any attachment.\n\n* feat(webmail): always display embedded images\n\nEmbedded images were hidden with the remote ones until the reader asked\nfor images, so most HTML messages opened with empty frames, logos and\nsignatures included. They travel with the message: displaying them\ntells no one it was read, unlike a remote image.\n\nThey are now always embedded as data: URIs, and only remote resources\nwait for the reader''s consent. When some were dropped, the message says\nso through remote_content_blocked, so the interface can offer to\ndisplay them rather than leave the reader to guess.\n\nReplies, forwards and drafts keep the previous behaviour: they would\ncarry the images as data: URIs, that many clients refuse to display.\nThe quarantine viewer shares the renderer and displays them too.\n\n* fix(webmail): let the listing fill the room under its toolbar\n\nThe list was positioned absolutely under a hardcoded 150px offset,\nwhich had to be kept in step with the height of everything above it,\nand a second offset when the scheduling notice was displayed.\n\nThe mailbox view is now a flex column: the toolbar and the notice take\ntheir own height, and the list fills what they leave and scrolls on\nits own. The toolbar becomes a v-toolbar, like the headers of the\nmessage and conversation views, which carries the surface, the height,\nthe border and the radius.\n\n* fix(webmail): indent the messages of an expanded conversation less\n\nThey started under the sender of their thread, which pushed the names\nmuch too far to the right. They now start under the control that\nrevealed them, and the sender cell takes back the difference, so\nsubjects and dates stay on the columns of the rows above.\n\n* feat(webmail): open a conversation of one message as a message\n\nThe message view says more about a single message than a conversation\nof one panel would. The listing opens it directly, and the conversation\nview hands over to the message view when the thread only has one\nmessage left, keeping its skeleton until then.\n\n* refactor(webmail): load conversation bodies from the open panels\n\nBodies were loaded from two places: once for the panels expanded on\narrival, and on the selection event of each panel for the ones the\nreader opens. They now follow the model of the accordion itself, one\nsource of truth whatever opened the panel.\n\n* feat(webmail): redesign the message and conversation headers\n\nThe subject becomes the title of a sticky header, with the date of the\nmessage or the size of the conversation next to it, rather than a\nheading stranded under a separate bar of buttons. Reply, reply all and\nforward are all visible, instead of two of them hiding behind a menu,\nand the other actions keep their meaning through the colour of their\nicon rather than a filled block.\n\nThe conversation is a flat accordion on the same surface as the\nlisting, with hairline separators. The flags and the date of each\nmessage keep a fixed width, so they line up from one message to the\nnext, and an expanded message can also be replied to all.\n\n* feat(webmail): show attachments as cards\n\nAttachments were a comma separated list of links. They are now a row\nof cards, each showing what kind of file it is, its name and its\napproximate size, and the whole card downloads it. A collapsed message\nof a conversation names its first file and counts the others, enough\nto find what one came for without opening every message.\n\nDownloads go through a shared helper that keeps the link in the\ndocument and the object URL alive long enough: Firefox ignored the\nclick on a detached link, and revoking the URL right away could cancel\na download that had not started yet.\n\n* feat(webmail): offer to display blocked remote images\n\nWhen remote resources were dropped from a message, a notice above its\nbody says so and offers to display them, instead of leaving the reader\nto find the option in the settings menu or to guess that anything was\nmissing. Nothing is shown when the message had nothing to block.\n\nIn a conversation the choice is made message by message, and only the\nmessage concerned is loaded again.\n\n* feat(webmail): return every header the message view displays\n\nBcc and Reply-To were only fetched when replying to a message or\nediting a draft, so the message view had no way to show who was in\nblind copy of a sent message, or where answers would go.\n\nThe message also carries its date in full, in the localized format,\nnext to the compact one of the listing that leaves parts out.\n\n* feat(webmail): lay out the sender, recipients and attachments together\n\nThe header block was a stack without a common grid: the sender as a\nlarge heading, the date stranded in the bar above, carbon copies\nappended to the To line without a label, and the attachments right\nagainst the body.\n\nAn avatar now anchors the block, and everything lines up on a column\nto its right: the sender with its address and the date, one labelled\nrow per kind of recipient, folding past a few names, then the files.\nA hairline tells the block from the notices and the body below it.\n\nEvery address is the same text button, which opens the contact card\nand reads \"Me\" for the reader''s own address. The conversation view\nuses the same block for its expanded messages, which had no recipients\nat all, without the sender and the date its panel title already shows.\n\nThe attachment cards become buttons of the framework too, rather than\nnative buttons styled by hand.\n\n* fix(webmail): keep every view clear of the top menu\n\nThe top menu is fixed in the top right corner and takes no room of its\nown: pages leave it some by starting with a title line. Only the\nmailbox view had one in the webmail, so the end of the subject of a\nmessage, or the size of a conversation, went under the menu, and the\ncard jumped up a line when a message was opened from the listing.\n\nThe title now belongs to the webmail layout, through a header slot of\nthe connected view, and every view of the webmail starts under it,\nwhere the listing does. The views fill what the title leaves, so their\nown scrolling is unchanged. Other sections don''t use the slot and keep\ntheir layout.\n\n* fix(webmail): separate the listing mode from the pagination\n\nOnly the 4px gap of the toolbar stood between the mode toggle and the\npage range, which read as a single group. The same separator as the\none before the toggle now closes it.","tree":{"sha":"88b450907bbc7fa8d4e00aa6f268bcf311126b8b","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modoboa/modoboa/git/trees/88b450907bbc7fa8d4e00aa6f268bcf311126b8b"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/modoboa/modoboa/git/commits/d3826a587fa604e710b779c14199c04a6e43e487","verification":{"payload":"tree 88b450907bbc7fa8d4e00aa6f268bcf311126b8b\nparent a75d9fd63039713dc458c292206f44ac48261077\nauthor Antoine Nguyen <tonio@ngyn.org> 1790014601 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1790014601 +0200\n\nfeat(webmail): conversation view and message display redesign (#4212)\n\n* feat(webmail): list messages as threads\n\nAdd support for the IMAP THREAD extension (RFC 5256) so that messages\ncan be grouped into conversations:\n\n* `parse_thread_response()` flattens the nested reply, ignoring an\n  unexpected one instead of raising;\n* `threads_count()` ranks the threads with the order already computed\n  by `messages_count()`, which works with and without SORT;\n* `fetch_threads()` summarizes a page of threads, going through the\n  same FETCH code path as the flat listing;\n* two read-only endpoints, `threads` and `thread`, which report\n  `threading_supported` when the server lacks the extension.\n\nThe flat listing is left untouched.\n\n* feat(webmail): add a listing mode preference\n\nA new \"listing_mode\" user preference chooses between the flat listing\nand conversations. It defaults to \"flat\", so nothing changes for\nexisting users.\n\n* feat(webmail): switch the message list to conversations\n\nThe messages list can now group messages into conversations:\n\n* the message card moves to its own EmailListItem component, so that\n  the list doesn''t mix both modes;\n* ThreadListItem displays a conversation and expands it on demand;\n* a toggle in the toolbar switches modes and saves the preference. It\n  is disabled where conversations make no sense (drafts, scheduled\n  messages) or when the server lacks the THREAD extension.\n\nSelecting a conversation selects all its messages, so the existing bulk\nactions apply to the whole thread without any API change.\n\n* feat(webmail): add a conversation view\n\nClicking a conversation now opens every message of the thread, each in\nits own collapsible panel. Unread messages and the last one are\nexpanded right away; the others load their body when opened, which\nmarks them as read, as before.\n\nThe message body moves to an EmailMessageBody component: the single\niframe of EmailView was absolutely positioned and sized by hand against\nthe window, which can''t work with several messages. Each frame now sits\nin the normal flow and takes the height of its content.\n\nMeasuring that content needs the frame to be same origin, so\n''allow-same-origin'' joins the sandbox. Scripts stay forbidden by the\nsandbox and by the CSP, and a document that cannot execute anything\ncannot use that origin either.\n\n* fix(webmail): don''t group unrelated messages into a conversation\n\nTHREAD=REFERENCES follows the reply chain, then applies the last step\nof RFC 5256: root messages sharing the same base subject are merged\ninto a single thread. Two unrelated messages called \"Invoice\" ended up\nin the same conversation, which is not what mail clients display.\n\nAsk for THREAD=REFS instead, the same algorithm without that step, and\nkeep REFERENCES as a fallback for servers that lack it.\n\n* feat(webmail): make the messages list readable at a glance\n\nThe listing displayed one card per message, with full size controls and\nthe sender stacked above the subject: around 90px per message, nothing\naligned from one row to the next, and unread messages signalled only by\na bold font.\n\nIt is now a single flat list of 40px rows with fixed columns, so the\neye scans a column instead of redrawing a boundary at every message:\n\n* a coloured bar on the left edge marks what hasn''t been read;\n* sender, subject and date sit in their own column, dates in tabular\n  figures;\n* the message size leaves the row: the paperclip already says there is\n  an attachment, and the size is on the message itself;\n* dates keep only what tells messages apart, the time today, the\n  weekday this week, then the day and month, and the year only when it\n  isn''t the current one;\n* a conversation shows \"▸3\" and its first two participants instead of a\n  chip, a full list of names and an \"unread\" counter.\n\n* fix(webmail): widen the date column and show the conversation control\n\nThe date column was 76px wide and allowed to wrap, so the longest\nformats went to a second line. Measured in a browser, the widest real\ndate is 85px (\"Sat 12:00 p.m.\", English with a weekday and a 12 hour\nclock) and 84px in Russian; the column is now 104px and never wraps.\n\nThe number of messages of a conversation was plain text, with nothing\nsaying it could be clicked. It becomes a small button, and it now sits\nin a column of its own, always present: a thread holding a single\nmessage used to have no such cell at all, which shifted its subject\naway from the others.\n\n* fix(webmail): don''t render messages as conversations while loading\n\nReloading a page in conversation mode raised \"props.thread.uids is\nundefined\": the listing was fetched flat before the preference was\nread, then the preference switched the display to conversations while\nthose flat results were still on screen, and a message has no uids.\n\nWhat is displayed now follows the results that came back, not the\npreference. The first listing also waits for the preference instead of\nbeing fetched flat and immediately fetched again, which removes both\nthe flash and one request per page load.\n\n* feat(webmail): align the listing toolbar with the list\n\nThe toolbar was an elevated card holding a 64px v-toolbar and filled\ncolour blocks, above a list that had become a flat sheet of 40px rows:\ntwo different surfaces, and three saturated buttons shouting over a\nlist whose only colour is the unread bar.\n\nIt is now the same surface as the list, with the same radius and the\nsame hairline border and no elevation. Actions keep their meaning\nthrough the colour of their icon rather than a filled block, the\nsearch field drops its outline for a filled field, and the page range\nis set in tabular figures.\n\nIts checkbox is padded by the width of the accent bar and one gap, so\nit sits exactly above the checkboxes of the rows.\n\n* feat(webmail): show the bulk actions only when messages are selected\n\nDelete, junk and the flag menu were always on display although they do\nnothing until something is selected. They now take the place of the\nsearch field while a selection is on, next to the number of selected\nmessages, and the search field comes back when it is cleared.\n\nThe mode toggle and the page controls keep their place in all cases,\nso nothing moves under the pointer. Emptying a mailbox leaves that\nmenu: it acts on the folder, not on a selection, so it becomes a\nbutton of its own, only in the trash.\n\nThe checkbox also reports a partial selection, and the selection is\ndropped when changing page or mailbox, where it would have applied to\nmessages that are no longer displayed.\n\n* feat(webmail): widen the listing search field\n\nThe field kept the 280px it had when the bulk actions sat next to it.\nIt now takes the room they left, up to 480px, past which a search\nfield stops being readable, and never goes below 180px.\n\n* feat(webmail): describe attachments from the body structure\n\nAttachments were only known by name, decoded with a loop that matched\nany parameter starting with \"name\" and ignored RFC 2231: accented or\nlong file names, split into encoded sections, came out garbled.\n\nThe MIME parameters are now decoded properly (RFC 2047 words, RFC 2231\ncharsets and numbered sections), and each attachment is described by\nits name, size and content type, all read from the body structure. The\nlisting already fetches it, so every message now carries its\nattachment list without its body being loaded.\n\n* fix(webmail): stop listing embedded images as attachments\n\nWhen a message was a multipart/related at its top level, the most\ncommon shape of an HTML message with a logo or a signature, the\nsubtype was lost on the way down to its parts: its images were listed\nas attachments and their cid: references were never resolved.\n\nParts are also sorted on more than their type. A text part is an\nattachment when its disposition says so, and an image is embedded when\nit can be displayed and carries a Content-ID, whether it sits in a\nmultipart/related or is marked inline in a multipart/mixed, as some\nclients do. Anything else, a PDF inside a multipart/related included,\nis an attachment.\n\nOnce the body is loaded, the embedded images it doesn''t reference, or\nall of them when the plain text is displayed, are listed with the\nattachments: they could not be seen otherwise. They can be downloaded\nlike any attachment.\n\n* feat(webmail): always display embedded images\n\nEmbedded images were hidden with the remote ones until the reader asked\nfor images, so most HTML messages opened with empty frames, logos and\nsignatures included. They travel with the message: displaying them\ntells no one it was read, unlike a remote image.\n\nThey are now always embedded as data: URIs, and only remote resources\nwait for the reader''s consent. When some were dropped, the message says\nso through remote_content_blocked, so the interface can offer to\ndisplay them rather than leave the reader to guess.\n\nReplies, forwards and drafts keep the previous behaviour: they would\ncarry the images as data: URIs, that many clients refuse to display.\nThe quarantine viewer shares the renderer and displays them too.\n\n* fix(webmail): let the listing fill the room under its toolbar\n\nThe list was positioned absolutely under a hardcoded 150px offset,\nwhich had to be kept in step with the height of everything above it,\nand a second offset when the scheduling notice was displayed.\n\nThe mailbox view is now a flex column: the toolbar and the notice take\ntheir own height, and the list fills what they leave and scrolls on\nits own. The toolbar becomes a v-toolbar, like the headers of the\nmessage and conversation views, which carries the surface, the height,\nthe border and the radius.\n\n* fix(webmail): indent the messages of an expanded conversation less\n\nThey started under the sender of their thread, which pushed the names\nmuch too far to the right. They now start under the control that\nrevealed them, and the sender cell takes back the difference, so\nsubjects and dates stay on the columns of the rows above.\n\n* feat(webmail): open a conversation of one message as a message\n\nThe message view says more about a single message than a conversation\nof one panel would. The listing opens it directly, and the conversation\nview hands over to the message view when the thread only has one\nmessage left, keeping its skeleton until then.\n\n* refactor(webmail): load conversation bodies from the open panels\n\nBodies were loaded from two places: once for the panels expanded on\narrival, and on the selection event of each panel for the ones the\nreader opens. They now follow the model of the accordion itself, one\nsource of truth whatever opened the panel.\n\n* feat(webmail): redesign the message and conversation headers\n\nThe subject becomes the title of a sticky header, with the date of the\nmessage or the size of the conversation next to it, rather than a\nheading stranded under a separate bar of buttons. Reply, reply all and\nforward are all visible, instead of two of them hiding behind a menu,\nand the other actions keep their meaning through the colour of their\nicon rather than a filled block.\n\nThe conversation is a flat accordion on the same surface as the\nlisting, with hairline separators. The flags and the date of each\nmessage keep a fixed width, so they line up from one message to the\nnext, and an expanded message can also be replied to all.\n\n* feat(webmail): show attachments as cards\n\nAttachments were a comma separated list of links. They are now a row\nof cards, each showing what kind of file it is, its name and its\napproximate size, and the whole card downloads it. A collapsed message\nof a conversation names its first file and counts the others, enough\nto find what one came for without opening every message.\n\nDownloads go through a shared helper that keeps the link in the\ndocument and the object URL alive long enough: Firefox ignored the\nclick on a detached link, and revoking the URL right away could cancel\na download that had not started yet.\n\n* feat(webmail): offer to display blocked remote images\n\nWhen remote resources were dropped from a message, a notice above its\nbody says so and offers to display them, instead of leaving the reader\nto find the option in the settings menu or to guess that anything was\nmissing. Nothing is shown when the message had nothing to block.\n\nIn a conversation the choice is made message by message, and only the\nmessage concerned is loaded again.\n\n* feat(webmail): return every header the message view displays\n\nBcc and Reply-To were only fetched when replying to a message or\nediting a draft, so the message view had no way to show who was in\nblind copy of a sent message, or where answers would go.\n\nThe message also carries its date in full, in the localized format,\nnext to the compact one of the listing that leaves parts out.\n\n* feat(webmail): lay out the sender, recipients and attachments together\n\nThe header block was a stack without a common grid: the sender as a\nlarge heading, the date stranded in the bar above, carbon copies\nappended to the To line without a label, and the attachments right\nagainst the body.\n\nAn avatar now anchors the block, and everything lines up on a column\nto its right: the sender with its address and the date, one labelled\nrow per kind of recipient, folding past a few names, then the files.\nA hairline tells the block from the notices and the body below it.\n\nEvery address is the same text button, which opens the contact card\nand reads \"Me\" for the reader''s own address. The conversation view\nuses the same block for its expanded messages, which had no recipients\nat all, without the sender and the date its panel title already shows.\n\nThe attachment cards become buttons of the framework too, rather than\nnative buttons styled by hand.\n\n* fix(webmail): keep every view clear of the top menu\n\nThe top menu is fixed in the top right corner and takes no room of its\nown: pages leave it some by starting with a title line. Only the\nmailbox view had one in the webmail, so the end of the subject of a\nmessage, or the size of a conversation, went under the menu, and the\ncard jumped up a line when a message was opened from the listing.\n\nThe title now belongs to the webmail layout, through a header slot of\nthe connected view, and every view of the webmail starts under it,\nwhere the listing does. The views fill what the title leaves, so their\nown scrolling is unchanged. Other sections don''t use the slot and keep\ntheir layout.\n\n* fix(webmail): separate the listing mode from the pagination\n\nOnly the 4px gap of the toolbar stood between the mode toggle and the\npage range, which read as a single group. The same separator as the\none before the toggle now closes it.","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqsXSJCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAApxMQAG5vZpShwgHpIWQ+PGxS+7mA\nyo6C1CgsFYHtjs92Cvdw7Gb2eP7tVyP3Ms2czSBUHkf7cL12XO7bxRBpQPxrqpPA\nekiKlbEaLsRewASHjs6Af4EngYUK9hRqLw53NgsIoGMOUqc1lfOKrxdr4ukIsJJX\nRymPeIjiZQ3QFYJynFRNMCC2Y9ej9rJzBe1TNrxmLP42iI4ophasJocYorGub10n\nqEVxDZU6NNwo3TR4qBMbepCRgcAhH+ZDOLW79s7w0POl2wcBp12uh0l44s3BMGU/\nqE7VqwgplfUTMITMHgMyx4MT9sn+CVWZSbZ4+NfelqeiCN6nuSDVQDwWM4Ya11wg\nMevtymrdG5PLP8SVlX6SioxebJLgKuut2Wr9lPHi9PNCht0pEl1bpucMi4ljAaBI\nmHD/Zu+ctvypN/fo+pruwZ6hKUhw5h30+jZM3ZvjJm3u1RB5Y+10JaDHo7TqzgL9\nF6lNHLAO66En7/i/5HFAtt6eX89+1gwZ5JVQZ+yUYt+hwaKwQ+kfQj9Uzs4LHj/e\nsG4zdG03O8QeOV6FjUVIU6NVhI588Fr7Hj4Bs40NyBSEvNSCC6ChAiU6xlMCObKE\n1UdbvnfucyRdks2H9w5XWALMGH7T9wcPyBxRl/gmrZ0A8r6jWIzXPwFNoiXEgKuD\nZRRdMMUHjuKxTID28QeB\n=ZU/K\n-----END PGP SIGNATURE-----\n","verified":true,"verified_at":"2026-09-21T18:16:41Z"}},"committer"